Use RefactorConstants.h.pkg
Use RefactorFileIO.pkg
Use cEncodingHelpers.pkg         // rfHtmlEscape and the encoding helpers
Use JsonEnhancement.pkg          // MemberEnh / MemberValueEnh - the .sws.lock and a package's .sws

/*  cLibraryInventory - what shared libraries does a workspace use, and where do they stand
    against the DataFlex version we are moving it to?

    A workspace's [Libraries] entries point at OTHER workspaces - vwin32fh, VDFQuery, SigCJ, DUF,
    RDCToolsLib, DFAbout - which are maintained upstream and must never be refactored in place. A
    copy inside a workspace is downstream of a repo, so editing it forks the library: the next
    update either clobbers the edits or conflicts with them. That holds for RDC's OWN libraries
    too - RDCToolsLib is fixed in Library-RDCToolsLib and re-released, not in a customer's copy.

    A STANDALONE CLASS, deliberately, not more functions on cRefactorApplication (2026-07-31).
    UnitTests declares "Object oApplication is a cApplication" - it does NOT use
    cRefactorApplication - so anything living there is unreachable from the suite: every
    "Get <fn> of ghoApplication" silently returns nothing and the tests pass or fail for reasons
    that have nothing to do with the logic. cDeepScanner is the pattern to copy: created on
    demand, tested directly.

    Local facts only. Whether upstream has something newer is a separate (network) question and
    deliberately not asked here, so this stays fast, offline and unit-testable.
*/
/*  A pinned dependency's identity: the .sws file stem, folder and extension removed.

    ONE SPELLING, because two things must agree on it - the Libraries dialog labels its rows with
    this and WorkspaceUpdateScan labels its findings with it, and a row whose key does not match
    the scan's shows an Update chip that never lights. It was _PackageKeyOf on the dialog until
    2026-09-10; it lives HERE, beside tSwsDependency, rather than beside the scan, because the
    scan's file drags the whole FlexTron chain and a pure function behind that is one no console
    test can call.

    Forward slashes first: a dependency records "StudioLibrary/vWin32fh-Library-DF26.0.sws" the
    JSON way, and a workspace on disk says the same thing with a backslash. */
Function SwsDependencyKey Global tSwsDependency Dep Returns String
    String sStem
    Integer iPos

    Move (Trim(Dep.sSws)) to sStem
    Move (Replaces("/", sStem, "\")) to sStem
    Move (RightPos("\", sStem)) to iPos
    If (iPos > 0) ;
        Move (Right(sStem, (Length(sStem) - iPos))) to sStem
    If (Right(Lowercase(sStem), 4) = ".sws") ;
        Move (Left(sStem, Length(sStem) - 4)) to sStem
    Function_Return sStem
End_Function

/* ---- WHAT KIND OF PIN A GIT DEPENDENCY HOLDS (2026-09-25) --------------------------------------

   The RDC libraries moved from commit pins to version tags, and a git dependency's "version" is
   now one of three things, each with its own answer to "is something newer?":

     a COMMIT       40 hex characters. Newer means the branch head has moved past it.
     a VERSION TAG  "1.0.0". Newer means the repository has published a later tag. A LIBRARY asks
                    for a range ("^1.0.0") and an APPLICATION pins the exact tag, listed before the
                    libraries that use it - so this is what an application's Update Check meets.
     anything else  a range ("^1.0.0", ">=1.0.0 <2.0.0") or a branch. There is no one pinned version
                    to compare it with, so the check leaves it alone rather than guess.

   Measured with df-cli 1.0.0 against a local tag fixture: a later range accepts an installed tag
   that satisfies it, an exact pin must equal it, and ONE commit pin anywhere on a tagged package
   breaks the workspace - a commit is not a version, so no range can accept it. That last fact is
   why the Libraries dialog must never write a commit where a tag was. */
Function IsGitCommitPin Global String sVersion Returns Boolean
    String sV sCh
    Integer i

    Move (Lowercase(Trim(sVersion))) to sV
    If (Length(sV) <> 40) ;
        Function_Return False
    For i from 1 to 40
        Move (Mid(sV, 1, i)) to sCh
        If (Pos(sCh, "0123456789abcdef") = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
    Loop
    Function_Return True
End_Function

/*  "1.2.3" -> 1, 2 and 3, answering True. PLAIN MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH ONLY: no "v" in front, no
    pre-release or build part after. That is how the RDC libraries tag a release and what df-cli
    matches a caret range against; a tag of any other shape ("Autumn2024", "0.1", "v1.0.0") is not a
    release this code offers, and a pin of that shape is not one it compares. */
Function VersionTagParts Global String sTag Integer ByRef iMajor Integer ByRef iMinor Integer ByRef iPatch Returns Boolean
    String[] asParts
    Integer[] aiPart
    String sPart sCh
    Integer i j iLen

    Move 0 to iMajor
    Move 0 to iMinor
    Move 0 to iPatch
    Move (StrSplitToArray((Trim(sTag)), ".")) to asParts
    If (SizeOfArray(asParts) <> 3) ;
        Function_Return False
    For i from 0 to 2
        Move asParts[i] to sPart
        Move (Length(sPart)) to iLen
        // Nine digits at most, so no part can overflow an Integer.
        If ((iLen = 0) or (iLen > 9)) ;
            Function_Return False
        For j from 1 to iLen
            Move (Mid(sPart, 1, j)) to sCh
            If (Pos(sCh, "0123456789") = 0) ;
                Function_Return False
        Loop
        Move sPart to aiPart[i]
    Loop
    Move aiPart[0] to iMajor
    Move aiPart[1] to iMinor
    Move aiPart[2] to iPatch
    Function_Return True
End_Function

// A pin on one release, "1.0.0" - VersionTagParts says what counts.
Function IsVersionTagPin Global String sVersion Returns Boolean
    Integer iMajor iMinor iPatch
    Function_Return (VersionTagParts(sVersion, &iMajor, &iMinor, &iPatch))
End_Function

/*  The newest published release a caret on the pinned one still accepts - or "" when there is none
    above the pin, or the pin is not a version tag.

    THE CARET, NOT THE NEWEST: the same major version, and for a 0.x pin the same minor too (0.x
    releases promise nothing). That is narrower than the newest tag on purpose. The libraries that
    use a package ask for "^<version>", so a pin moved to the NEXT major is one they refuse, and
    df-cli then reports the whole workspace BROKEN. Measured 2026-09-25: `package upgrade` with
    nothing in its way took a "1.0.0" pin straight to 2.0.0; with a library's ^1.0.0 in the way it
    stopped at 1.0.1. Moving to a new major is a decision somebody makes, not an update to offer.
    Versions compare as NUMBERS - 1.10.0 is above 1.2.0. */
Function NewestCompatibleVersionTag Global String sPinned String[] asTags Returns String
    String sBest
    Integer iPinMajor iPinMinor iPinPatch iMajor iMinor iPatch iBestMajor iBestMinor iBestPatch
    Integer i iMax
    Boolean bInside bAbove

    If ((VersionTagParts(sPinned, &iPinMajor, &iPinMinor, &iPinPatch)) = False) ;
        Function_Return ""
    Move ""        to sBest
    Move iPinMajor to iBestMajor
    Move iPinMinor to iBestMinor
    Move iPinPatch to iBestPatch
    Move (SizeOfArray(asTags) - 1) to iMax
    For i from 0 to iMax
        If (VersionTagParts(asTags[i], &iMajor, &iMinor, &iPatch)) Begin
            // Inside the caret: the same major; for 0.x the same minor; a 0.0.x pin cannot move.
            Move (iMajor = iPinMajor) to bInside
            If ((bInside = True) and (iPinMajor = 0)) ;
                Move ((iMinor = iPinMinor) and ((iPinMinor > 0) or (iPatch = iPinPatch))) to bInside
            Move ((iMajor > iBestMajor) or ((iMajor = iBestMajor) and ((iMinor > iBestMinor) or ((iMinor = iBestMinor) and (iPatch > iBestPatch))))) to bAbove
            If ((bInside = True) and (bAbove = True)) Begin
                Move (Trim(asTags[i])) to sBest
                Move iMajor to iBestMajor
                Move iMinor to iBestMinor
                Move iPatch to iBestPatch
            End
        End
    Loop
    Function_Return sBest
End_Function

/*  The tag names out of `git ls-remote --tags --refs <url>`, one "<sha><TAB>refs/tags/<name>" a line.

    True when the output is that shape throughout - and an EMPTY output is a real answer: the
    repository has no tags. False when anything else is there (a "fatal:" line, a credential
    prompt, a proxy's error page), and False means UNKNOWN, never "nothing newer" - a failed
    question must not come back as "up to date". A peeled "^{}" line is skipped in case --refs was
    not honoured. Pure, so the console runner tests it without a network. */
Function GitTagsFromLsRemote Global String[] asLines String[] ByRef asTags Returns Boolean
    String[] asOut asNone
    String sLine sRef
    Integer i iMax iPos

    Move asNone to asTags
    Move (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1) to iMax
    For i from 0 to iMax
        Move (Trim(asLines[i])) to sLine
        If (sLine <> "") Begin
            // A space instead of the tab, if a shell normalised it.
            Move (Pos(Character(9), sLine)) to iPos
            If (iPos = 0) ;
                Move (Pos(" ", sLine)) to iPos
            If (iPos <> 41) ;
                Function_Return False
            If ((IsGitCommitPin(Left(sLine, 40))) = False) ;
                Function_Return False
            Move (Trim(Right(sLine, (Length(sLine) - iPos)))) to sRef
            If (Left(sRef, 10) <> "refs/tags/") ;
                Function_Return False
            Move (Right(sRef, (Length(sRef) - 10))) to sRef
            If ((sRef <> "") and (Right(sRef, 3) <> "^{}")) ;
                Move sRef to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
        End
    Loop
    Move asOut to asTags
    Function_Return True
End_Function

/* ---- THE PROGRAMS AN INI .sws LISTS -------------------------------------------------------------

   Nils 2026-09-11: after a workspace was converted to the DataFlex 26 JSON format, its most
   important program was missing from the Compile program list.

   Its [Projects] section had TWO lines keyed Project1 and none keyed Project0. Nothing enforces
   the numbering - a .sws is hand-editable, and this one had been edited - and every reader that
   asks for a KEY loses one of the two. A first-wins reader keeps the first line; df-cli's convert
   keeps the LAST and drops the other without a word. Measured 2026-09-11 on a scratch copy of that
   file: the dropped program is not even among the ones df-cli reports it could not find, and the
   same list with its keys renumbered Project0..N converts whole.

   So these read LINES, never keys, and both INI writers renumber the section before anything
   key-based sees it: the copy step (NewSwsLinesDropping) and the Libraries dialog's conversion
   (oConvertWorkspace_dg). GetWorkspaceProjects reads its list through them, and both df-cli
   conversions check afterwards that every program arrived (SwsProgramsMissing).

   GLOBAL rather than methods, for two reasons: the console runner calls a global with nothing to
   create, and GetWorkspaceProjects is reachable from a web combo's OnFill, where creating an object
   hangs the WebView2 pump. Defined in calling order - a function used in an expression must already
   be known to the compiler. */

// The number in a "Project<n>" key, or -1 for any other key. Case-insensitive, and only digits
// after the word: "ProjectPath", or "Project" alone, is not a program slot.
Function SwsIniProjectKeyNumber Global String sKey Returns Integer
    String sLow sDigits
    Integer iChar iMax iNum

    Move (Lowercase(Trim(sKey))) to sLow
    If (Left(sLow, 7) <> "project" or Length(sLow) < 8) ;
        Function_Return -1
    Move (Right(sLow, (Length(sLow) - 7))) to sDigits
    Move (Length(sDigits)) to iMax
    For iChar from 1 to iMax
        If (Pos(Mid(sDigits, 1, iChar), "0123456789") = 0) ;
            Function_Return -1
    Loop
    // No workspace has a slot past nine digits. One that claims to is CLAMPED, not dropped, so its
    // program still sorts - last - rather than vanishing.
    If (iMax > 9) ;
        Function_Return 999999999
    Move sDigits to iNum
    Function_Return iNum
End_Function

/* Per LINE of an INI .sws: the slot number when the line is a Project<n> entry inside [Projects],
   else -1 - with that entry's trimmed value in asValues at the same index. ONE classification, so
   the list, the "is it in order?" test and the rewrite cannot disagree about which lines are
   program lines. Blank lines, ";" and "//" comments, any other key, and every line outside
   [Projects] are -1. CR and tabs are stripped before trimming, as SwsSectionEntries does, because
   Trim removes spaces only. */
Function SwsIniProjectSlots Global String[] asLines String[] ByRef asValues Returns Integer[]
    Integer[] aiSlots
    String sLine
    Integer i iMax iPos
    Boolean bIn

    Move (ResizeArray(asValues, 0)) to asValues
    Move False to bIn
    Move (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1) to iMax
    For i from 0 to iMax
        Move -1 to aiSlots[i]
        Move "" to asValues[i]
        Move (Replaces(Character(13), asLines[i], "")) to sLine
        Move (Replaces(Character(9), sLine, " ")) to sLine
        Move (Trim(sLine)) to sLine
        If (Left(sLine, 1) = "[") ;
            Move (Lowercase(sLine) = "[projects]") to bIn
        Else If (bIn = True and Left(sLine, 1) <> ";" and Left(sLine, 2) <> "//") Begin
            Move (Pos("=", sLine)) to iPos
            If (iPos > 1) Begin
                Move (SwsIniProjectKeyNumber(Left(sLine, (iPos - 1)))) to aiSlots[i]
                If (aiSlots[i] <> -1) ;
                    Move (Trim(Right(sLine, (Length(sLine) - iPos)))) to asValues[i]
            End
        End
    Loop
    Function_Return aiSlots
End_Function

/* The PROGRAMS an INI .sws lists, every one, in the order a numbered reader shows them: by slot
   number, and a REPEATED number keeps both entries in the order they were written - the case every
   key-based reader gets wrong. The same program under two slots is listed once, compared
   case-insensitively because these are Windows file names; a slot with no value is no program. */
Function SwsIniProjectsFromLines Global String[] asLines Returns String[]
    Integer[] aiSlots
    String[] asValues asKeyed asSeenLow asOut
    String sValue sLow
    Integer i iMax

    Move (SwsIniProjectSlots(asLines, &asValues)) to aiSlots
    Move (SizeOfArray(aiSlots) - 1) to iMax
    For i from 0 to iMax
        // The sort key: nine digits of slot number, then seven of line position, then the value.
        // The sort is decided by that fixed-width prefix alone, so a tie keeps its written order.
        If (aiSlots[i] <> -1 and asValues[i] <> "") ;
            Move (Right(("000000000" + String(aiSlots[i])), 9) + Right(("0000000" + String(i)), 7) + asValues[i]) to asKeyed[SizeOfArray(asKeyed)]
    Loop
    Move (SortArray(asKeyed)) to asKeyed
    Move (SizeOfArray(asKeyed) - 1) to iMax
    For i from 0 to iMax
        Move (Right(asKeyed[i], (Length(asKeyed[i]) - 16))) to sValue
        Move (Lowercase(sValue)) to sLow
        If (SearchArray(sLow, asSeenLow) = -1) Begin
            Move sLow   to asSeenLow[SizeOfArray(asSeenLow)]
            Move sValue to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
        End
    Loop
    Function_Return asOut
End_Function

/* Does [Projects] need rewriting? False when its program lines, in file order, are already slots
   0, 1, 2 ... holding exactly SwsIniProjectsFromLines' list - which is every healthy file, and every
   file with no [Projects] at all, a JSON .sws included. Spacing inside a line does not count: a
   healthy file is never rewritten for looks. */
Function SwsIniProjectsNeedRenumbering Global String[] asLines Returns Boolean
    Integer[] aiSlots
    String[] asValues asPrograms
    Integer i iMax iNext

    Move (SwsIniProjectSlots(asLines, &asValues)) to aiSlots
    Move (SwsIniProjectsFromLines(asLines)) to asPrograms
    Move 0 to iNext
    Move (SizeOfArray(aiSlots) - 1) to iMax
    For i from 0 to iMax
        If (aiSlots[i] <> -1) Begin
            If (aiSlots[i] <> iNext or iNext >= SizeOfArray(asPrograms)) ;
                Function_Return True
            If (asValues[i] <> asPrograms[iNext]) ;
                Function_Return True
            Increment iNext
        End
    Loop
    Function_Return False
End_Function

/* The .sws LINES with every program slot in [Projects] rewritten Project0..N, in the order
   SwsIniProjectsFromLines gives, where the first slot line stood. Every other line - other keys,
   comments, blank lines, every other section - passes through exactly as it was read, and lines
   that need no renumbering come back untouched. */
Function SwsIniLinesWithProjectsRenumbered Global String[] asLines Returns String[]
    Integer[] aiSlots
    String[] asValues asPrograms asOut
    Integer i iMax iProg iProgMax
    Boolean bPlaced

    If (SwsIniProjectsNeedRenumbering(asLines) = False) ;
        Function_Return asLines
    Move (SwsIniProjectSlots(asLines, &asValues)) to aiSlots
    Move (SwsIniProjectsFromLines(asLines)) to asPrograms
    Move (SizeOfArray(asPrograms) - 1) to iProgMax
    Move False to bPlaced
    Move (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1) to iMax
    For i from 0 to iMax
        If (aiSlots[i] = -1) ;
            Move asLines[i] to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
        Else If (bPlaced = False) Begin
            For iProg from 0 to iProgMax
                Move ("Project" + String(iProg) + "=" + asPrograms[iProg]) to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
            Loop
            Move True to bPlaced
        End
    Loop
    Function_Return asOut
End_Function

/* Renumber [Projects] in the INI .sws sSwsFile, in place, when it needs it. False only when a
   needed write failed; nothing to do - a healthy file, a JSON one, a missing one - answers True and
   the file is not touched. The file keeps its shape: its BOM, its line breaks and its final line
   break are read and written back as they were. */
Function SwsIniRenumberProjectsInFile Global String sSwsFile Returns Boolean
    String[] asLines asOut
    String sLineBreak
    Boolean bTrailing bUtf8

    If (Trim(sSwsFile) = "") ;
        Function_Return True
    If (vFilePathExists(sSwsFile) = 0) ;
        Function_Return True
    Move (rfReadEncodedFileLines(sSwsFile, False, &bTrailing)) to asLines
    If (SwsIniProjectsNeedRenumbering(asLines) = False) ;
        Function_Return True
    Move (SwsIniLinesWithProjectsRenumbered(asLines)) to asOut
    Move (rfFileHasBOM(sSwsFile)) to bUtf8
    Move (rfDetectFileLineBreak(sSwsFile)) to sLineBreak
    Function_Return (rfWriteEncodedFileFromStringArrayEx(&asOut, sSwsFile, sLineBreak, bUtf8, bTrailing))
End_Function

/* The programs in asListed that asNow does not list, in asListed's order - compared
   case-insensitively, as file names are. What a conversion is checked with: the list the old file
   carried against the list the new one answers. */
Function SwsProgramsMissing Global String[] asListed String[] asNow Returns String[]
    String[] asNowLow asOut
    String sLow
    Integer i iMax

    Move (SizeOfArray(asNow) - 1) to iMax
    For i from 0 to iMax
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(asNow[i]))) to asNowLow[i]
    Loop
    Move (SizeOfArray(asListed) - 1) to iMax
    For i from 0 to iMax
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(asListed[i]))) to sLow
        If (sLow <> "" and SearchArray(sLow, asNowLow) = -1) ;
            Move asListed[i] to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
    Loop
    Function_Return asOut
End_Function

// The words for SwsProgramsMissing's answer, "" when nothing is missing - one wording for both
// conversions, the Libraries dialog's and the migration copy's.
Function SwsProgramsMissingNote Global String[] asLost Returns String
    String sList
    Integer i iMax

    Move (SizeOfArray(asLost) - 1) to iMax
    If (iMax < 0) ;
        Function_Return ""
    Move "" to sList
    For i from 0 to iMax
        If (i > 0) ;
            Move (sList + ", ") to sList
        Move (sList + asLost[i]) to sList
    Loop
    Function_Return (String(iMax + 1) + " program(s) the old workspace file listed did not survive the conversion: " + sList + ". Add them back to the " + '"projects"' + " list in the .sws, or they will be missing from Compile program.")
End_Function

/*  The last iMaxLines non-blank lines of a captured tool run as one indented block - "" when it
    said nothing - for a report that quotes df-cli under its own verdict (the copy step, 2026-09-14).
    The LAST lines, because df-cli states its decision at the end and can spend the lines before it
    dumping a file it choked on. iMaxLines 0 = all of them. Pure; lives beside the other report
    wording so the test runner can reach it.

    THE .cfg NOTES ARE LEFT OUT AND COUNTED (Nils 2026-10-29, LegacyApp5_20). df-cli opens a package
    command with "Config file for project 'X' could not be found in workspace '...', using defaults."
    for every listed program that has no .cfg - thirteen of them on that workspace. They are not
    errors, df-cli carries on with the defaults; but the Libraries dialog quoted the FIRST fifteen
    lines, so they filled the box and the real reason (an incompatible vWin32fh ref) was cut off.
    Dropped from the quote, and one line says how many, so a run that printed nothing else still
    does not read as silent. */
Function DfCliOutputNote Global String[] ByRef asLines Integer iMaxLines Returns String
    String[] asKeep
    String sOut sLine
    Integer i iFirst iCfgNotes

    Move 0 to iCfgNotes
    For i from 0 to (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1)
        Move (Trim(asLines[i])) to sLine
        If (Pos("Config file for project ", sLine) = 1 and Pos("using defaults.", sLine) > 0) ;
            Increment iCfgNotes
        Else If (sLine <> "") ;
            Move sLine to asKeep[SizeOfArray(asKeep)]
    Loop
    If (SizeOfArray(asKeep) = 0 and iCfgNotes = 0) ;
        Function_Return ""
    Move 0 to iFirst
    If (iMaxLines > 0 and SizeOfArray(asKeep) > iMaxLines) ;
        Move (SizeOfArray(asKeep) - iMaxLines) to iFirst
    Move "" to sOut
    For i from iFirst to (SizeOfArray(asKeep) - 1)
        Move (sOut + Character(10) + "   " + asKeep[i]) to sOut
    Loop
    If (iCfgNotes > 0) ;
        Move (sOut + Character(10) + "   (" + String(iCfgNotes) + " line(s) left out that only say a program has no .cfg file - df-cli uses the defaults for it, so they are not the problem.)") to sOut
    Function_Return sOut
End_Function

/*  df-cli's "Incompatible ref for <package>: <a> does not match <b>", taken apart - False when the
    output has no such line. Shared by the plain-words hint below and by StaleLockPin, which needs
    the same three values to decide whether the lock file is where the conflict comes from. */
Function DfCliIncompatibleRef Global String[] ByRef asLines String ByRef sPkg String ByRef sAsked String ByRef sOther Returns Boolean
    String sLine
    Integer i iAt iColon iMatch

    For i from 0 to (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1)
        Move (Trim(asLines[i])) to sLine
        Move (Pos("Incompatible ref for ", sLine)) to iAt
        If (iAt > 0) Begin
            // "Incompatible ref for " is 21 characters, " does not match " 16.
            Move (Right(sLine, Length(sLine) - iAt - 20)) to sLine
            Move (Pos(": ", sLine)) to iColon
            Move (Pos(" does not match ", sLine)) to iMatch
            If (iColon > 1 and iMatch > (iColon + 2)) Begin
                Move (Left(sLine, iColon - 1)) to sPkg
                Move (Mid(sLine, iMatch - iColon - 2, iColon + 2)) to sAsked
                Move (Trim(Right(sLine, Length(sLine) - iMatch - 15))) to sOther
                If (sOther <> "") ;
                    Function_Return True
            End
        End
    Loop
    Function_Return False
End_Function

/*  One plain paragraph for df-cli's "Incompatible ref for <package>: <a> does not match <b>" - ""
    when the output has no such line (Nils 2026-10-29, LegacyApp5_20). Two routes through the workspace
    ask for one package by different refs, and df-cli treats a version and a git commit as
    different even when the tag sits on that very commit. On LegacyApp5_20 the commit was a leftover in
    the .sws.lock from an earlier DUF pin - nothing in the dependency chain asked for it any more.
    Which of the two is the old one is not said: df-cli's line does not say either. Pure, beside
    DfCliOutputNote for the same reason. */
Function DfCliIncompatibleRefHint Global String[] ByRef asLines Returns String
    String sPkg sAsked sOther
    Boolean bFound

    Get DfCliIncompatibleRef (&asLines) (&sPkg) (&sAsked) (&sOther) to bFound
    If (bFound = False) ;
        Function_Return ""
    Function_Return ("Why: two parts of this workspace ask for " + sPkg + " in different ways - as " + sAsked + " and as " + sOther + ". df-cli treats a version number and a git commit as different, even when both are the same code. One of the two can be a leftover in the workspace's .sws.lock file from an earlier version of a dependency.")
End_Function

/*  Does a .sws.lock key name the package df-cli calls sPkgName ("NilsSve/vWin32fh-Library-DF26.0")?
    A git key is "<host>/<owner>/<repo>.git/<.sws path without .sws>" -
    github.com/NilsSve/Library-vwin32fh.git/StudioLibrary/vWin32fh-Library-DF26.0 - and df-cli's name
    is the owner and the .sws stem. A registry key ("DataFlex-dev/Web UI") has no ".git/" and is
    never a match: df-cli's incompatible REF is about a git package. Case-blind, as URLs are. */
Function LockKeyNamesPackage Global String sKey String sPkgName Returns Boolean
    String sOwner sStem
    Integer iPos

    Move (Lowercase(Trim(sKey))) to sKey
    Move (Lowercase(Trim(sPkgName))) to sPkgName
    Move (Pos("/", sPkgName)) to iPos
    If ((iPos < 2) or (iPos = Length(sPkgName)) or (Pos(".git/", sKey) = 0)) ;
        Function_Return False
    Move (Left(sPkgName, iPos - 1)) to sOwner
    Move (Right(sPkgName, Length(sPkgName) - iPos)) to sStem
    Function_Return ((Right(sKey, Length(sStem) + 1) = ("/" + sStem)) and (Pos(("/" + sOwner + "/"), sKey) > 0))
End_Function

/*  The entries of a .sws.lock's text, in file order: each key and its "ref" - the full transitive
    set df-cli resolved, which the .sws itself never lists. Returns how many; 0 for "" or a lock
    without a dependencies member. */
Function LockFileEntries Global String sLockJson String[] ByRef asKeys String[] ByRef asRefs Returns Integer
    Handle hoJson hoDeps hoDep
    String[] asNone
    String sKey sRef
    Integer i iMax
    Boolean bOk

    Move asNone to asKeys
    Move asNone to asRefs
    If (Trim(sLockJson) = "") ;
        Function_Return 0
    Get Create (RefClass(cJsonObject)) to hoJson
    Get ParseString of hoJson sLockJson to bOk
    If (bOk = True) Begin
        Set pbRequireAllMembers of hoJson to False
        Get MemberEnh of hoJson "dependencies" to hoDeps
        If (hoDeps <> 0) Begin
            Get MemberCount of hoDeps to iMax
            For i from 0 to (iMax - 1)
                Get MemberNameByIndex of hoDeps i to sKey
                Move "" to sRef
                Get MemberEnh of hoDeps sKey to hoDep
                If (hoDep <> 0) Begin
                    Set pbRequireAllMembers of hoDep to False
                    Get MemberValueEnh of hoDep "ref" to sRef
                    Send Destroy of hoDep
                End
                Move sKey         to asKeys[SizeOfArray(asKeys)]
                Move (Trim(sRef)) to asRefs[SizeOfArray(asRefs)]
            Loop
            Send Destroy of hoDeps
        End
    End
    Send Destroy of hoJson
    Function_Return (SizeOfArray(asKeys))
End_Function

/*  Does this JSON .sws text ask for sPkgName at exactly sRef? Each "dependencies" object is read:
    its source names the owner, its "sws" the stem (SwsDependencyKey), and its "version" must be
    sRef as written. A range ("^1.0.0") is NOT an ask for the tag or commit it happens to resolve
    to - that difference is the conflict being looked at. */
Function SwsJsonAsksForRef Global String sSwsJson String sPkgName String sRef Returns Boolean
    Handle hoJson hoDeps hoItem
    tSwsDependency Dep
    String sOwner sStem
    Integer i iMax iPos iType
    Boolean bOk bHit

    Move (Lowercase(Trim(sPkgName))) to sPkgName
    Move (Lowercase(Trim(sRef))) to sRef
    Move (Pos("/", sPkgName)) to iPos
    If ((iPos < 2) or (sRef = "") or (Trim(sSwsJson) = "")) ;
        Function_Return False
    Move (Left(sPkgName, iPos - 1)) to sOwner
    Move (Right(sPkgName, Length(sPkgName) - iPos)) to sStem
    Move False to bHit
    Get Create (RefClass(cJsonObject)) to hoJson
    Get ParseString of hoJson sSwsJson to bOk
    If (bOk = True) Begin
        Set pbRequireAllMembers of hoJson to False
        Get MemberEnh of hoJson "dependencies" to hoDeps
        If (hoDeps <> 0) Begin
            Get MemberCount of hoDeps to iMax
            For i from 0 to (iMax - 1)
                Get MemberJsonTypeEnh of hoDeps i to iType
                If ((bHit = False) and (iType = jsonTypeObject)) Begin
                    Get MemberEnh of hoDeps i to hoItem
                    If (hoItem <> 0) Begin
                        Set pbRequireAllMembers of hoItem to False
                        Get MemberValueEnh of hoItem "source"  to Dep.sSource
                        Get MemberValueEnh of hoItem "sws"     to Dep.sSws
                        Get MemberValueEnh of hoItem "version" to Dep.sVersion
                        Send Destroy of hoItem
                        If ((Lowercase(SwsDependencyKey(Dep)) = sStem) and (Pos(("/" + sOwner + "/"), Lowercase(Dep.sSource)) > 0) ;
                            and (Lowercase(Trim(Dep.sVersion)) = sRef)) ;
                            Move True to bHit
                    End
                End
            Loop
            Send Destroy of hoDeps
        End
    End
    Send Destroy of hoJson
    Function_Return bHit
End_Function

Class cLibraryInventory is a cObject

    Procedure Construct_Object
        Forward Send Construct_Object
    End_Procedure

    /* Raw "key=value" lines of one INI section, read as TEXT.

       Not cIniFile: this class must work in every host program including the console test
       runner, and a .sws is a handful of short lines - reading it with the file I/O the rest of
       the codebase already uses costs nothing and keeps the logic testable.

       Section match is case-insensitive; blank lines and ';' / '//' comments are skipped;
       reading stops at the next '[' header. CR and tabs are stripped before trimming because
       DataFlex's Trim removes spaces only. */
    Function SwsSectionEntries String sSwsFile String sSection Returns String[]
        String[] asLines asEntries
        String sLine sLow sWant
        Integer i iMax
        Boolean bIn

        If (vFilePathExists(sSwsFile) = 0) ;
            Function_Return asEntries

        Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sSwsFile)) to asLines
        Move (Lowercase("[" + Trim(sSection) + "]")) to sWant
        Move False to bIn
        Move (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (Replaces(Character(13), asLines[i], "")) to sLine
            Move (Replaces(Character(9), sLine, " ")) to sLine
            Move (Trim(sLine)) to sLine
            Move (Lowercase(sLine)) to sLow
            If (Left(sLine, 1) = "[") Begin
                // A header either opens the section we want or closes it.
                Move (sLow = sWant) to bIn
            End
            Else Begin
                If (bIn = True and sLine <> "" and Left(sLine, 1) <> ";" and Left(sLine, 2) <> "//") Begin
                    If (Pos("=", sLine) > 0) ;
                        Move sLine to asEntries[SizeOfArray(asEntries)]
                End
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return asEntries
    End_Function

    // The value of one key in one INI section, "" when absent. Case-insensitive on the key.
    Function SwsSectionValue String sSwsFile String sSection String sKey Returns String
        String[] asEntries
        String sEntry sName
        Integer i iMax iPos

        Get SwsSectionEntries sSwsFile sSection to asEntries
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sKey))) to sKey
        Move (SizeOfArray(asEntries) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move asEntries[i] to sEntry
            Move (Pos("=", sEntry)) to iPos
            Move (Lowercase(Trim(Left(sEntry, (iPos - 1))))) to sName
            If (sName = sKey) ;
                Function_Return (Trim(Mid(sEntry, (Length(sEntry) - iPos), (iPos + 1))))
        Loop
        Function_Return ""
    End_Function

    /* ---- .sws / .ws PATH PLUMBING ---------------------------------------------------------------
       Moved down here from cWorkspaceCopyPlan (2026-08-06) so every subclass inherits ONE
       implementation. They are all facts about reading a workspace file, which is this class's job;
       leaving them one level up meant cWorkspaceFile could not fill its Paths without either a
       second copy or an upward dependency. Defined ABOVE their callers - a class function used in
       EXPRESSION form must already be known to the compiler. */

    // Is sPath already absolute ("C:\..." or a "\\server\share" UNC)?
    Function _IsAbsolutePath String sPath Returns Boolean
        If (sPath = "") ;
            Function_Return False
        If (Mid(sPath, 1, 2) = ":") ;
            Function_Return True
        If (Left(sPath, 2) = "\\") ;
            Function_Return True
        Function_Return False
    End_Function

    // Local copy of cRefactorApplication.RemoveFolderSeperator - these classes must work in the
    // console test runner, which has no cRefactorApplication to send to.
    Function RemoveFolderSeperator String sPath Returns String
        If (Right(sPath, 1) = "\") ;
            Function_Return (Left(sPath, Length(sPath) - 1))
        Function_Return sPath
    End_Function

    // The folder holding sFile, WITH its trailing separator. Deliberately local rather than
    // RDCToolsLib's global ParseFolderName, for the same runner reason.
    Function _FolderOf String sFile Returns String
        Integer iPos
        Move (RightPos("\", sFile)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 0) ;
            Function_Return (Left(sFile, iPos))
        Function_Return ""
    End_Function

    /* Collapses "." and ".." segments ANYWHERE in a path, so two strings naming the same folder
       compare equal as strings.

       WHY THIS IS NOT COSMETIC. Windows resolves ".." itself when it opens a file, which is
       exactly why its absence hid for so long: "C:\Proj\LegacyApp10\..\Libraries\vdfquery" opens the right
       folder every single time. It then fails every prefix comparison against
       "C:\Proj\Libraries\..." - silently, because a prefix test that returns False looks just like
       a path that genuinely is not underneath - and prints itself to the user verbatim. It reached
       a dialog as "E:\LegacyApp10\..\Libraries\vdfquery" on 2026-08-06, which is how it was finally caught.

       The root prefix is held aside so ".." can never eat a drive letter or a UNC server\share. A
       leading ".." on a RELATIVE path has nothing to pop and is KEPT: that is the only honest
       answer, since the caller has not given us enough to resolve it. */
    Function NormalizePath String sPath Returns String
        String[] asParts asOut
        String sPrefix sRest sSeg sJoined
        Integer i iMax iPos iKeep
        Boolean bAppend

        Move (Trim(sPath)) to sPath
        If (sPath = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (Replaces("/", sPath, "\")) to sPath

        Move "" to sPrefix
        Move sPath to sRest
        If (Left(sPath, 2) = "\\") Begin
            // UNC: "\\server\share" is the root, and both halves must survive intact.
            Move (Mid(sPath, (Length(sPath) - 2), 3)) to sRest
            Move (Pos("\", sRest)) to iPos
            If (iPos = 0) ;
                Function_Return sPath
            Move ("\\" + Left(sRest, iPos - 1)) to sPrefix
            Move (Mid(sRest, (Length(sRest) - iPos), (iPos + 1))) to sRest
            Move (Pos("\", sRest)) to iPos
            If (iPos > 0) Begin
                Move (sPrefix + "\" + Left(sRest, iPos - 1)) to sPrefix
                Move (Mid(sRest, (Length(sRest) - iPos), (iPos + 1))) to sRest
            End
            Else Begin
                Move (sPrefix + "\" + sRest) to sPrefix
                Move "" to sRest
            End
        End
        Else If (Mid(sPath, 1, 2) = ":") Begin
            Move (Left(sPath, 2)) to sPrefix
            Move (Mid(sPath, (Length(sPath) - 2), 3)) to sRest
            If (Left(sRest, 1) = "\") ;
                Move (Mid(sRest, (Length(sRest) - 1), 2)) to sRest
        End

        Move (StrSplitToArray(sRest, "\")) to asParts
        Move (SizeOfArray(asParts) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (Trim(asParts[i])) to sSeg
            If (sSeg <> "" and sSeg <> ".") Begin
                If (sSeg = "..") Begin
                    Move True to bAppend
                    Move (SizeOfArray(asOut)) to iKeep
                    If (iKeep > 0) Begin
                        // Only pop a real folder name. Popping a ".." would turn "..\..\X" into
                        // "X", quietly moving the path two levels DOWN from where it belongs.
                        If (asOut[iKeep - 1] <> "..") Begin
                            Move (ResizeArray(asOut, iKeep - 1)) to asOut
                            Move False to bAppend
                        End
                    End
                    // There is nothing above a ROOT - Windows resolves "C:\A\..\..\B" to "C:\B" -
                    // so a ".." with nothing left to pop is discarded. On a RELATIVE path it is
                    // kept instead, because we have not been told what it is relative to.
                    If (sPrefix <> "" and SizeOfArray(asOut) = 0) ;
                        Move False to bAppend
                    If (bAppend = True) ;
                        Move sSeg to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                End
                Else Begin
                    Move sSeg to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                End
            End
        Loop

        Move "" to sJoined
        Move (SizeOfArray(asOut) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (sJoined = "") Begin
                Move asOut[i] to sJoined
            End
            Else Begin
                Move (sJoined + "\" + asOut[i]) to sJoined
            End
        Loop
        If (sPrefix = "") ;
            Function_Return sJoined
        If (sJoined = "") ;
            Function_Return (sPrefix + "\")
        Function_Return (sPrefix + "\" + sJoined)
    End_Function

    // Resolve sRelative against sBase, collapsing any leading "..\" and ".\" segments. DataFlex
    // workspace files use both ("Home=..\", "AppSrcPath=.\AppSrc"), and the two are anchored to
    // DIFFERENT bases - see WorkspaceHomeFromConfig.
    Function _ResolvePath String sBase String sRelative Returns String
        String sPath sRest
        Integer iPos

        Move (Trim(sRelative)) to sRest
        If (sRest = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        If (_IsAbsolutePath(Self, sRest)) ;
            Function_Return (NormalizePath(Self, sRest))

        Move (vFolderFormat(sBase)) to sPath
        // Walk off the leading "..\" / ".\" segments, stepping sPath up one folder per "..".
        Move True to iPos
        While (iPos > 0)
            If (Left(sRest, 3) = "..\") Begin
                Move (Mid(sRest, (Length(sRest)), 4)) to sRest
                Move (RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, sPath)) to sPath
                Move (RightPos("\", sPath)) to iPos
                If (iPos > 0) ;
                    Move (Left(sPath, iPos)) to sPath
            End
            Else If (Left(sRest, 2) = ".\") Begin
                Move (Mid(sRest, (Length(sRest)), 3)) to sRest
            End
            Else Begin
                Move 0 to iPos
            End
        Loop
        Move (vFolderFormat(sPath) + sRest) to sPath
        // Normalise on the way out, not just the leading segments walked off above: an entry may
        // carry ".." in the MIDDLE ("Libraries\..\Shared\X.sws"), and an ABSOLUTE entry skips the
        // walk entirely, so without this the two paths that name one folder still differ.
        Function_Return (NormalizePath(Self, RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, sPath)))
    End_Function

    // The legacy .ws config file the .sws delegates its folder paths to, or "" when the .sws
    // declares none (a JSON workspace carries its paths inline). Relative to the .sws's own folder.
    Function WorkspaceConfigFile String sSwsFile Returns String
        String sValue sHome sPath

        If (sSwsFile = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Get SwsSectionValue sSwsFile "WorkspacePaths" "ConfigFile" to sValue
        If (sValue = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Get _FolderOf sSwsFile to sHome
        Get _ResolvePath sHome sValue to sPath
        Function_Return sPath
    End_Function

    /* The workspace HOME, from the .ws [Workspace] Home key.

       ANCHORING, which is easy to get wrong: "Home" is relative to the .ws FILE's own folder (the
       .ws sits in Programs\, so "Home=..\" means the folder above Programs\), while every other
       path key is relative to HOME. Resolving both against the same base puts AppSrc inside
       Programs. */
    Function WorkspaceHomeFromConfig String sWsFile Returns String
        String sValue sBase sHome

        If (sWsFile = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Get SwsSectionValue sWsFile "Workspace" "Home" to sValue
        Get _FolderOf sWsFile to sBase
        If (sValue = "") ;
            Function_Return (RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, sBase))
        Get _ResolvePath sBase sValue to sHome
        Function_Return sHome
    End_Function

    /* Which on-disk shape a .sws has - eSwsIni or eSwsJson; eSwsMissing for no file at all, and
       eSwsConflict for a file carrying git conflict markers - decided from its CONTENT.

       Lives here, on the class that already parses .sws files, so every subclass inherits one
       answer. Format is a fact about the FILE and nothing else: not its name, not its extension
       (".sws" either way), and not the DataFlex version. The DF26 Studio converts a WORKSPACE .sws
       to JSON when it migrates one but leaves LIBRARY .sws files as INI - verified 2026-08-05, 1
       JSON against 103 INI under DF26 - so a single workspace routinely references files of both
       shapes at once, and any per-version guess would be wrong half the time.

       A JSON document's first non-blank character is "{"; an INI .sws opens with a "[Section]"
       header. Only the first few lines are read: this is asked before every read of a .sws, and
       parsing a whole document to learn how to parse it would be a poor trade. */
    Function SwsFormatOf String sSwsFile Returns Integer
        String[] asLines
        String sLine
        Integer i iMax

        // Missing is its OWN answer - see eSwsMissing. Returning eSwsIni here made
        // "no workspace" indistinguishable from "old workspace" at every call site.
        If (Trim(sSwsFile) = "" or vFilePathExists(sSwsFile) = 0) ;
            Function_Return eSwsMissing
        Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sSwsFile)) to asLines
        Move (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1) to iMax
        // CONFLICT MARKERS FIRST, over the whole file (a .sws is a handful of lines): an unfinished
        // merge is neither format whatever its first line says - see eSwsConflict (2026-09-14).
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (Left(asLines[i], 8) = "<<<<<<< ") ;
                Function_Return eSwsConflict
        Loop
        If (iMax > 12) ;
            Move 12 to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (Trim(Replaces(Character(13), asLines[i], ""))) to sLine
            If (sLine <> "") Begin
                If (Left(sLine, 1) = "{") ;
                    Function_Return eSwsJson
                Function_Return eSwsIni
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return eSwsIni
    End_Function

    /* The DFRefactor-OWN .sws candidate in sHome, PREFERRING the format the caller can read as
       its own (eWantFormat = eSwsJson / eSwsIni). THE AMBIGUITY WAS REAL, not hypothetical:
       until this repo went DataFlex 26 only, home held DFRefactor25.0.sws (INI, for the DF25
       Studio) NEXT TO DFRefactorFlexTron.sws (JSON, DF26) - and "the first DFRefactor* found"
       picked the DF25 file on the DF26 build (an
       alphabetical accident), handing TestBench's Test Compile a workspace with no projects
       array and a version the DF26 toolchain refuses (Nils 2026-08-28: "always says the program
       compiled clean"). A PREFERENCE, not a filter: when only an other-format candidate exists
       it is still returned - the caller's own parse guards decide what to do with it - and a
       folder holding exactly ONE .sws returns that file whatever its name or format, exactly as
       before. eWantFormat is a parameter rather than an #IF here so the rule is testable from
       the DFUnit runner, whatever version that happens to be built with. */
    Function OwnSwsCandidateInFolder String sHome Integer eWantFormat Returns String
        tRFSearchResult[] aFiles
        String sOnly sPreferred sOther sLeaf sFull
        Integer i iMax iPos iCount iFormat

        Move (rfFileSearch((vFolderFormat(sHome) + "*.sws"), DIRMODE_FILES_ONLY, False)) to aFiles
        Move (SizeOfArray(aFiles) - 1) to iMax
        Move 0 to iCount
        Move "" to sOnly
        Move "" to sPreferred
        Move "" to sOther
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move aFiles[i].sFilename to sLeaf
            Move (RightPos("\", sLeaf)) to iPos
            If (iPos > 0) ;
                Move (Right(sLeaf, (Length(sLeaf) - iPos))) to sLeaf
            Move (vFolderFormat(sHome) + sLeaf) to sFull
            Increment iCount
            Move sFull to sOnly
            If (Uppercase(Left(sLeaf, 10)) = "DFREFACTOR") Begin
                Get SwsFormatOf sFull to iFormat
                If (iFormat = eWantFormat) Begin
                    If (sPreferred = "") ;
                        Move sFull to sPreferred
                End
                Else Begin
                    If (sOther = "") ;
                        Move sFull to sOther
                End
            End
        Loop
        If (iCount = 1) ;
            Function_Return sOnly
        If (sPreferred <> "") ;
            Function_Return sPreferred
        Function_Return sOther
    End_Function

    /* The DataFlex version a .sws declares for ITSELF, e.g. "19.1", from its [Properties] block.

       This is the ONLY reliable way to version a .sws. The 2026-07-30 survey found four
       incompatible filename schemes across four libraries - DFUnit-25.0, DFAbout25.0,
       vWin32fh-Library-DF191 (undotted), VDFQuery182 - and libraries that CHANGE their own scheme
       between releases (vwin32fh DF191 -> DF25.0; DbUpdateFramework 19.1 -> DUFLibrary25.0). The
       [Properties] block is uniform everywhere. Never parse the filename.

       JSON-format .sws (DF26+) carry the same fact elsewhere; ReadSwsJsonFile lives on
       cRefactorApplication and is asked for it in the real app via SwsJsonVersion below. */
    Function SwsDataFlexVersion String sSwsFile Returns String
        String sVersion

        If (vFilePathExists(sSwsFile) = 0) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Get SwsJsonVersion sSwsFile to sVersion
        If (sVersion <> "") ;
            Function_Return sVersion
        Get SwsSectionValue sSwsFile "Properties" "Version" to sVersion
        Function_Return (Trim(sVersion))
    End_Function

    /* HOOK for JSON-format .sws (DataFlex 26+), which carry the version as JSON rather than an
       INI [Properties] block. Returns "" today, so SwsDataFlexVersion falls through to the INI
       reader.

       Deliberately NOT wired to cRefactorApplication.ReadSwsJsonFile: that would put a
       ghoApplication reference inside this class, which reintroduces exactly the coupling this
       class exists to avoid - and a compile-order dependency besides. Every workspace and library
       .sws on this machine is INI-format today (the 2026-07-30 survey read 235 of them), and
       vwin32fh's own DF26 support arrived as a commit titled "Adding a df26 sws file (ini
       format)". Override this in a subclass, or pass the parsed version in, when a JSON .sws
       actually turns up. */
    Function SwsJsonVersion String sSwsFile Returns String
        Function_Return ""
    End_Function

    /* A DataFlex version string as a COMPARABLE integer: "19.1" -> 19001, "25.0" -> 25000.
       0 means "could not read it", which callers must treat as "no answer" rather than as zero.

       Integer parts, never a Number. Converting "19.1" to a Number goes through the locale's
       decimal separator, so on a machine set to Swedish the dot is not a decimal point and the
       value that comes back is not the one in the file - and every comparison built on it is
       quietly wrong ([[feedback_dataflex_locale_decimal_separator]]). Two integers around the dot
       have no separator to disagree about.

       Only the leading digits of each part are read, so a "25.0 RC1" or a stray space cannot make
       the whole version unreadable. */
    { Visibility=Private }
    Function _VersionKey String sVersion Returns Integer
        String sMajor sMinor sCh
        Integer i iPos iLen

        Move (Trim(sVersion)) to sVersion
        If (sVersion = "") ;
            Function_Return 0
        Move (Pos(".", sVersion)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 0) Begin
            Move (Left(sVersion, (iPos - 1))) to sMajor
            Move (Mid(sVersion, (Length(sVersion) - iPos), (iPos + 1))) to sMinor
        End
        Else Begin
            Move sVersion to sMajor
            Move "0" to sMinor
        End

        // Keep the leading digits of each part and drop whatever follows.
        Move "" to sCh
        Move (Length(sMajor)) to iLen
        For i from 1 to iLen
            If (Mid(sMajor, 1, i) >= "0" and Mid(sMajor, 1, i) <= "9") ;
                Move (sCh + Mid(sMajor, 1, i)) to sCh
            Else ;
                Move iLen to i
        Loop
        Move sCh to sMajor
        Move "" to sCh
        Move (Length(sMinor)) to iLen
        For i from 1 to iLen
            If (Mid(sMinor, 1, i) >= "0" and Mid(sMinor, 1, i) <= "9") ;
                Move (sCh + Mid(sMinor, 1, i)) to sCh
            Else ;
                Move iLen to i
        Loop
        Move sCh to sMinor

        If (sMajor = "") ;
            Function_Return 0
        If (sMinor = "") ;
            Move "0" to sMinor
        Function_Return ((Integer(sMajor) * 1000) + Integer(sMinor))
    End_Function

    /* Is sVersion OLDER than sTarget? The one place that question is answered, so the report, the
       maintenance dialog and anything later all mean the same thing by "behind".

       NUMERIC, because the strings do not sort: "9.0" compares GREATER than "19.1" as text, which
       is the exact shape of a library copy that predates the two-digit era and would be reported as
       up to date.

       FALSE WHENEVER THERE IS NO ANSWER - a blank version, a blank target, or something neither
       side can parse. "Behind" is a claim about the user's workspace and it drives a colour they
       will act on; making it on a version nobody could read would be a guess wearing a fact's
       clothes. A version AHEAD of the target (DFUnit ships the sentinel "99.0") is not behind
       either, which is what keeps that sentinel from needing a guard of its own. */
    /* Does sTarget fall inside the [sMin, sMax] release range a package declares?

       BOTH ENDS ARE OPTIONAL and an absent end means "no bound that way", not zero: a package
       stating only df-min supports everything from there on. A range with neither end says nothing
       and cannot answer - which is why the caller has to ask HasRange first rather than reading
       False as a refusal.

       Inclusive at both ends: "df-min 26.0, df-max 26.0" is DataFlex 26's package, and the whole
       point of a max is to include the release it names.

       Numeric through _VersionKey, for the same reason IsVersionBelow is - "9.0" sorts above "19.1"
       as text. Unreadable input at either end is treated as "no bound", never as a limit that
       happens to exclude everything. */
    Function VersionInRange String sTarget String sMin String sMax Returns Boolean
        Integer iTarget iMin iMax

        Get _VersionKey sTarget to iTarget
        If (iTarget = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get _VersionKey sMin to iMin
        Get _VersionKey sMax to iMax
        If (iMin > 0 and iTarget < iMin) ;
            Function_Return False
        If (iMax > 0 and iTarget > iMax) ;
            Function_Return False
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    // Is there a usable range at all? "No range" and "a range that excludes you" are different
    // answers and only one of them is a verdict about the package.
    Function HasVersionRange String sMin String sMax Returns Boolean
        Integer iMin iMax
        Get _VersionKey sMin to iMin
        Get _VersionKey sMax to iMax
        Function_Return (iMin > 0 or iMax > 0)
    End_Function

    /* HOOK: the df-min / df-max a PACKAGE declares in its own cached df.sws.

       Stubbed to "" here for the same reason the other JSON hooks are - reading it needs
       cJsonObject through cRefactorApplication.ReadSwsJsonFile, and a ghoApplication reference in
       this class would make it unusable from the console runner. cLibraryInventoryApp fills it in.

       A host without that subclass sees no range, and every package row correctly falls back to
       "managed by df-cli" with no verdict. */
    Procedure SwsPackageDfRange String sSwsFile String ByRef sMin String ByRef sMax
        Move "" to sMin
        Move "" to sMax
    End_Procedure

    Function IsVersionBelow String sVersion String sTarget Returns Boolean
        Integer iVer iTarget

        Get _VersionKey sVersion to iVer
        Get _VersionKey sTarget  to iTarget
        If (iVer = 0 or iTarget = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Function_Return (iVer < iTarget)
    End_Function

    /* HOOK for a JSON-format .sws (DataFlex 26+), whose libraries are its "dependencies" array.

       Returns "" here, exactly like SwsJsonVersion above and for the same reason: parsing JSON
       needs cJsonObject and the workspace reader that owns it (cRefactorApplication.ReadSwsJsonFile,
       which already handles both dependency shapes), and reaching for ghoApplication from this class
       would break the console test runner - it declares a plain cApplication, so every
       "of ghoApplication" would silently return nothing.

       cLibraryInventoryApp (cFTLibraryReport.pkg) overrides this where the app object exists. Any
       host without that subclass sees a JSON workspace as having no libraries, which is why
       SwsLibraryEntries below is the ONLY caller - one place to be wrong, and one place to fix. */
    Function SwsJsonLibraryEntries String sSwsFile Returns String[]
        String[] asNone
        Function_Return asNone
    End_Function

    /* How many dependencies are the OBJECT form ({source, version, sws}) - a git or file dependency
       that SwsJsonLibraryEntries deliberately leaves out, because it has no path to resolve and no
       version to read off disk.

       Counted rather than ignored. Dropping rows without saying so is precisely how the old library
       report came to describe a six-library workspace as having four; a caller can now state the
       omission instead of quietly shortening its own list. Same hook rule as the two above - 0 here,
       answered by cLibraryInventoryApp where the JSON reader lives. */
    Function SwsJsonObjectDependencyCount String sSwsFile Returns Integer
        Function_Return 0
    End_Function

    /* THE OBJECT DEPENDENCIES THEMSELVES, not just how many (2026-08-14).

       Counting them was enough while nothing here could act on one. Now that a package can be
       ADDED (AddPackageFT) and removed (df-cli package uninstall), a count is the wrong shape of
       answer - the Libraries dialog has to show each one and let it be selected.

       Same hook rule as its neighbours: empty here, answered by cLibraryInventoryApp where the
       JSON reader lives, so the console test runner is not dragged into needing ghoApplication. */
    Function SwsJsonObjectDependencies String sSwsFile Returns tSwsDependency[]
        tSwsDependency[] aNone
        Function_Return aNone
    End_Function

    /* The workspace OVERRIDES - what this workspace says a dependency should really be.

       DataFlex 26 lets a workspace override a version or an alternate .sws for something one of
       its LIBRARIES declares, and "only root-workspace overrides take effect" (tSwsFile.aOverrides).
       That matters on screen: a card can otherwise state the version a library declares while the
       build uses another one entirely, which is a readout quietly disagreeing with the compiler.

       Same hook rule as its neighbours - empty here, answered by cLibraryInventoryApp. */
    Function SwsJsonOverrides String sSwsFile Returns tSwsOverride[]
        tSwsOverride[] aNone
        Function_Return aNone
    End_Function

    /* The RAW library entries of a .sws, in file order - the paths as written, unresolved.

       FORMAT-BLIND at the call site (2026-08-12). Every screen and every resolver asks this one
       question; whether the answer comes from an INI [Libraries] section or a JSON "dependencies"
       array is this function's business, not theirs. Before, an INI-only reader meant a JSON
       workspace reported no libraries at all - true of nothing, and the shape of answer that reads
       as "you have none" rather than "I cannot tell". */
    Function SwsLibraryEntries String sSwsFile Returns String[]
        String[] asEntries asRaw
        String sEntry
        Integer i iMax iPos

        If (SwsFormatOf(Self, sSwsFile) = eSwsJson) ;
            Function_Return (SwsJsonLibraryEntries(Self, sSwsFile))

        Get SwsSectionEntries sSwsFile CS_Libraries to asEntries
        Move (SizeOfArray(asEntries) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move asEntries[i] to sEntry
            Move (Pos("=", sEntry)) to iPos
            Move (Trim(Mid(sEntry, (Length(sEntry) - iPos), (iPos + 1)))) to sEntry
            If (sEntry <> "") ;
                Move sEntry to asRaw[SizeOfArray(asRaw)]
        Loop
        Function_Return asRaw
    End_Function

    /* How an [Libraries] entry SHOULD be WRITTEN for a workspace whose home is sHomeFolder:
       relative when the library sits inside that home or beside it, absolute only when it is
       somewhere else entirely.

       NOT cosmetic, and not a preference. A relative entry is what makes a workspace PORTABLE -
       it survives being copied, cloned or junctioned, which is the whole premise of the migration
       flow this dialog sits in and of the shared library pool ("..\Libraries\X" resolves against
       the copy exactly as it did against the original). An absolute entry written for a library
       that is in fact enclosed would silently tie the copy back to the machine it was made on, and
       the failure is invisible until someone else opens it.

       THREE cases, in order:
         inside home              -> "Libraries\vwin32fh\StudioLibrary\vWin32fh-Library-DF25.0.sws"
         beside home (same parent)-> "..\Libraries\vwin32fh\...\...sws"   (the pool shape)
         anywhere else            -> the absolute path, unchanged - the honest answer, since
                                     nothing shorter would resolve.
       Comparison is on NORMALIZED, lower-cased strings (a raw "C:\P\LegacyApp10\..\Libraries" fails every
       prefix test while opening the right folder), but the RETURNED text keeps the caller's case. */
    Function RelativeLibraryEntry String sLibSwsFile String sHomeFolder Returns String
        String sLib sHome sParent sLibLow sHomeLow sParentLow
        Integer iPos

        Move (Trim(sLibSwsFile)) to sLib
        If (sLib = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Get NormalizePath sLib to sLib
        Get NormalizePath sHomeFolder to sHome
        Get RemoveFolderSeperator sHome to sHome
        If (sHome = "") ;
            Function_Return sLib

        Move (Lowercase(sLib))  to sLibLow
        Move (Lowercase(sHome)) to sHomeLow
        If (Left(sLibLow, (Length(sHomeLow) + 1)) = (sHomeLow + "\")) ;
            Function_Return (Mid(sLib, (Length(sLib) - Length(sHome) - 1), (Length(sHome) + 2)))

        // The folder holding home. A home AT a drive root has none, so the sibling case simply
        // does not apply and the absolute path stands.
        Move (RightPos("\", sHome)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 1) Begin
            Move (Left(sHome, (iPos - 1))) to sParent
            Move (Lowercase(sParent)) to sParentLow
            If (Left(sLibLow, (Length(sParentLow) + 1)) = (sParentLow + "\")) ;
                Function_Return ("..\" + Mid(sLib, (Length(sLib) - Length(sParent) - 1), (Length(sParent) + 2)))
        End

        Function_Return sLib
    End_Function

    /* REWRITE a .sws file's [Libraries] section to exactly asEntries, renumbered Lib1..LibN.

       The one WRITER against a .sws in this class, and the only place the Library Maintenance
       dialog's OK lands. Everything outside [Libraries] passes through byte-for-byte: this is
       surgery on one section, not a re-serialisation, so a workspace file keeps every key, comment
       and section this tool has never heard of.

       A JSON .sws GOES TO ITS OWN WRITER (_WriteSwsJsonDependencies), never through the INI logic
       below: that logic finds an INI section header and appends INI lines when it finds none, so
       run against a JSON document it would bolt "[Libraries]" onto the end and produce a file
       neither the Studio nor this tool can read. The two formats share this entry point and nothing
       else. Both splice ONE thing and pass every other byte through; either can still return False,
       and a caller must treat that as "not saved" rather than assume it wrote.

       THE SECTION HEADER IS WRITTEN WHEN IT IS ABSENT - the silent-failure case found on
       DataFolderSelector25.0.sws, where Lib1..Lib3 sat directly under [Projects] with no
       [Libraries] header: the Studio parses by section, so those lines were never loaded as
       libraries and the dependencies failed to resolve with no error anywhere. Adding entries to
       such a file without the header would reproduce exactly that bug. (Stray Lib lines already
       sitting under another section are LEFT ALONE - they are ignored by the Studio today and will
       go on being ignored; deleting keys out of a section we do not own is not this function's
       business.)

       An EMPTY asEntries keeps the [Libraries] header and empties it, rather than removing the
       section: "this workspace uses no libraries" is a statement worth being able to read in the
       file, and re-adding one then needs no structural change. */
    /* One entry as a JSON string literal, quotes included.

       Escapes what JSON requires and nothing else: backslash and double-quote. A Windows path is
       ALL backslashes, so an unescaped entry is not merely wrong - "C:\Proj" carries \P, an invalid
       escape, and the document stops parsing. Forward slashes (what df-cli itself writes) need no
       escaping and are left alone, so a hand-added absolute path and a df-cli-written relative one
       both survive.

       Backslash FIRST, or the backslashes introduced by the quote escape get escaped again. */
    { Visibility=Private }
    Function _JsonQuoted String sText Returns String
        String s
        Move (Replaces("\", sText, "\\")) to s
        Move (Replaces('"', s, '\"')) to s
        Function_Return ('"' + s + '"')
    End_Function

    /* REWRITE a JSON .sws file's "dependencies" array to exactly asEntries.

       TEXT SURGERY ON ONE KEY, not a parse-and-restringify - the same decision as the INI path above
       and for a stronger reason. A round trip through tSwsFile would re-serialise the whole
       document from a struct that models what this tool happens to know about, so every key it does
       not model is silently DROPPED and every one it does is reformatted. A .sws carries the
       Studio's class preferences, project debug arguments, overrides, package metadata - things this
       tool has no business rewriting to change a library list. Splicing one array leaves every other
       byte identical.

       The array is located by its KEY and closed by bracket DEPTH, so it copes with the object form
       of a dependency ({source, version, sws}, which spans lines) as well as plain path strings. It
       is deliberately conservative: anything it cannot recognise returns False, and the caller
       reports that the save did not happen rather than writing something it guessed at.

       INDENTATION is taken from the line the key sits on, so the rewritten array matches the
       document it lands in instead of announcing itself.

       NO "dependencies" KEY AT ALL is a legitimate file (a workspace with no libraries), so one is
       inserted after the opening brace when there are entries to write - and when there are none,
       there is nothing to do and the file is left exactly as it was. */
    { Visibility=Private }
    Function _WriteSwsJsonDependencies String sSwsFile String[] asEntries Returns Boolean
        String[] asLines asOut asElem asBlk asBLines
        String sLine sTrim sIndent sEntry sPad sNorm
        Integer i iMax iN iOut iKeyAt iOpenAt iCloseAt iDepth iPos iCh
        Integer e iElemMax b iBlkMax k iBLMax
        Boolean bOk bStringsDone

        Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sSwsFile)) to asLines
        Move (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1) to iMax
        If (iMax < 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (SizeOfArray(asEntries) - 1) to iN

        // ---- locate the key line -----------------------------------------------------------
        // Matched on the quoted KEY, then a colon - so both '"dependencies":' and the legal
        // '"dependencies" :' are found, and a "dependencies" appearing as a VALUE somewhere is not.
        Move -1 to iKeyAt
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (iKeyAt = -1) Begin
                Move (Trim(Replaces(Character(9), (Replaces(Character(13), asLines[i], "")), " "))) to sTrim
                If (Left(sTrim, 14) = '"dependencies"') Begin
                    Move (Trim(Mid(sTrim, (Length(sTrim) - 14), 15))) to sLine
                    If (Left(sLine, 1) = ":") ;
                        Move i to iKeyAt
                End
            End
        Loop

        If (iKeyAt = -1) Begin
            /* Absent. Nothing to remove means nothing to do; something to add means inserting the
               key immediately after the opening brace, which is a position no other key depends on
               (JSON objects are unordered) and which keeps the diff to the lines added. */
            If (iN < 0) ;
                Function_Return True
            Move 0 to iOpenAt
            For i from 0 to iMax
                Move (Trim(Replaces(Character(13), asLines[i], ""))) to sTrim
                If (iOpenAt = 0 and Left(sTrim, 1) = "{") ;
                    Move (i + 1) to iOpenAt
            Loop
            If (iOpenAt = 0 or iOpenAt > iMax) ;
                Function_Return False
            /* The inserted block ends with a comma, which is only correct when a member follows it.
               An object with nothing else in it would get a trailing comma and stop parsing, so a
               .sws that is an empty object is refused rather than damaged - it is not a workspace
               file this tool has any business writing libraries into. */
            Move (Trim(Replaces(Character(13), asLines[iOpenAt], ""))) to sTrim
            If (Left(sTrim, 1) = "}") ;
                Function_Return False
            For i from 0 to (iOpenAt - 1)
                Move asLines[i] to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
            Loop
            Move '    "dependencies": [' to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
            For iOut from 0 to iN
                Move (Trim(asEntries[iOut])) to sEntry
                If (sEntry <> "") Begin
                    Move ('        ' + _JsonQuoted(Self, sEntry)) to sLine
                    If (iOut < iN) ;
                        Move (sLine + ",") to sLine
                    Move sLine to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                End
            Loop
            Move '    ],' to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
            For i from iOpenAt to iMax
                Move asLines[i] to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
            Loop
            Get rfWriteEncodedFileFromStringArray (&asOut) sSwsFile C_NewLine False to bOk
            Function_Return bOk
        End

        // ---- find the closing bracket by DEPTH ---------------------------------------------
        // Counted across the whole span rather than assuming one element per line: an object-form
        // dependency spans several lines and carries its own braces.
        Move 0 to iDepth
        Move -1 to iCloseAt
        For i from iKeyAt to iMax
            Move (Replaces(Character(13), asLines[i], "")) to sLine
            Move (Length(sLine)) to iPos
            For iCh from 1 to iPos
                If (Mid(sLine, 1, iCh) = "[") ;
                    Increment iDepth
                If (Mid(sLine, 1, iCh) = "]") Begin
                    Move (iDepth - 1) to iDepth
                    If (iDepth = 0 and iCloseAt = -1) ;
                        Move i to iCloseAt
                End
            Loop
            If (iCloseAt <> -1) ;
                Move iMax to i
        Loop
        // An unbalanced or inline-but-unrecognisable array is refused rather than guessed at.
        If (iCloseAt = -1 or iDepth <> 0) ;
            Function_Return False

        /* GIT / CLOUD DEPENDENCIES ARE CARRIED OVER VERBATIM (Nils 2026-08-14).

           A DF26 dependency is either a PATH STRING or an OBJECT ({source, version, sws}) - a git
           or cloud package. SwsLibraryEntries only ever returns the strings, so asEntries can only
           ever describe the strings, and rebuilding the array from it alone would silently delete
           every package. This used to REFUSE the write for exactly that reason, which was honest
           but useless: the first workspace Nils converted to JSON has two git packages, so the
           Libraries dialog could not save it at all - every removal reported "not saved".

           So the array is taken apart into its top-level elements and put back together with the
           object elements copied out CHARACTER FOR CHARACTER, internal line breaks and all.
           Nothing this tool does not understand is reformatted, let alone dropped.

           The replacement strings go where the FIRST string element was, so a hand-ordered file
           keeps its shape; if there were no strings at all they go last. Order carries no meaning
           to the package handler - dependencies is a union - but a small diff is a readable one. */
        Get _JsonArrayElements (&asLines) iKeyAt iCloseAt to asElem

        // The key line's own indentation, so the rewritten block matches the document.
        Move asLines[iKeyAt] to sLine
        Move "" to sIndent
        Move (Length(sLine)) to iPos
        For iCh from 1 to iPos
            If (Mid(sLine, 1, iCh) = " " or Mid(sLine, 1, iCh) = Character(9)) ;
                Move (sIndent + Mid(sLine, 1, iCh)) to sIndent
            Else ;
                Move iPos to iCh
        Loop
        Move (sIndent + "    ") to sPad

        // ---- the blocks to emit, in order ---------------------------------------------------
        Move False to bStringsDone
        Move (SizeOfArray(asElem) - 1) to iElemMax
        For e from 0 to iElemMax
            // Line breaks folded to spaces for the TEST only - asElem[e] itself stays raw.
            Move (Trim(Replaces(Character(10), (Replaces(Character(9), asElem[e], " ")), " "))) to sNorm
            If (Left(sNorm, 1) = "{") ;
                Move asElem[e] to asBlk[SizeOfArray(asBlk)]
            Else Begin
                // A string element: the WHOLE run of them is replaced by asEntries, once, here.
                If (bStringsDone = False) Begin
                    If (iN >= 0) Begin
                        For iOut from 0 to iN
                            Move (Trim(asEntries[iOut])) to sEntry
                            If (sEntry <> "") ;
                                Move (_JsonQuoted(Self, sEntry)) to asBlk[SizeOfArray(asBlk)]
                        Loop
                    End
                    Move True to bStringsDone
                End
            End
        Loop
        // Nothing to replace means the entries are new: they go after whatever was there.
        If (bStringsDone = False and iN >= 0) Begin
            For iOut from 0 to iN
                Move (Trim(asEntries[iOut])) to sEntry
                If (sEntry <> "") ;
                    Move (_JsonQuoted(Self, sEntry)) to asBlk[SizeOfArray(asBlk)]
            Loop
        End

        // ---- rebuild: everything before, the new array, everything after --------------------
        For i from 0 to (iKeyAt - 1)
            Move asLines[i] to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
        Loop
        // A trailing comma on the CLOSING line survives, because the key may not be the last member
        // of its object - and a JSON document with a missing or doubled comma is unreadable.
        Move (Trim(Replaces(Character(13), asLines[iCloseAt], ""))) to sTrim
        Move (SizeOfArray(asBlk) - 1) to iBlkMax
        If (iBlkMax < 0) Begin
            Move (sIndent + '"dependencies": []') to sLine
            If (Right(sTrim, 1) = ",") ;
                Move (sLine + ",") to sLine
            Move sLine to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
        End
        Else Begin
            Move (sIndent + '"dependencies": [') to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
            For b from 0 to iBlkMax
                Get _SplitOnLf asBlk[b] to asBLines
                Move (SizeOfArray(asBLines) - 1) to iBLMax
                For k from 0 to iBLMax
                    // The first line is placed at OUR indent; the rest go out untouched, so a
                    // multi-line object keeps the internal shape it had in the file.
                    If (k = 0) ;
                        Move (sPad + Trim(asBLines[k])) to sLine
                    Else ;
                        Move asBLines[k] to sLine
                    // The separator belongs on the LAST line of a block, not on every line of one.
                    If (k = iBLMax and b < iBlkMax) ;
                        Move (sLine + ",") to sLine
                    Move sLine to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                Loop
            Loop
            Move (sIndent + "]") to sLine
            If (Right(sTrim, 1) = ",") ;
                Move (sLine + ",") to sLine
            Move sLine to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
        End

        For i from (iCloseAt + 1) to iMax
            Move asLines[i] to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
        Loop

        Get rfWriteEncodedFileFromStringArray (&asOut) sSwsFile C_NewLine False to bOk
        Function_Return bOk
    End_Function

    /* THE TOP-LEVEL ELEMENTS OF A JSON ARRAY, RAW.

       A DF26 dependency may be an object spanning several lines, so the elements cannot be found by
       splitting lines on commas - that cuts an object in half, and the halves look like two broken
       dependencies. This walks CHARACTERS and breaks only at a comma that is outside every brace,
       bracket and string literal.

       Each element comes back with its internal line breaks intact as Character(10), which is what
       lets the writer copy an object back out unchanged. Whitespace BETWEEN elements is separation
       and is dropped; nothing inside one is touched - trailing whitespace is held in sPend and only
       committed when a later non-blank character proves it was interior.

       iFrom/iTo span the key line to the array's closing bracket. Scanning starts after the first
       "[", so the key itself contributes nothing. */
    Function _JsonArrayElements String[] asLines Integer iFrom Integer iTo Returns String[]
        String[] asElem
        String sAll sCh sCur sPend
        Integer i iLen iCh iBrace iBrack
        Boolean bInStr bEsc bStarted bInElem

        Move "" to sAll
        For i from iFrom to iTo
            If (i > iFrom) ;
                Move (sAll + Character(10)) to sAll
            Move (sAll + Replaces(Character(13), asLines[i], "")) to sAll
        Loop

        Move "" to sCur
        Move "" to sPend
        Move 0 to iBrace
        Move 0 to iBrack
        Move False to bInStr
        Move False to bEsc
        Move False to bStarted
        Move False to bInElem
        Move (Length(sAll)) to iLen
        For iCh from 1 to iLen
            Move (Mid(sAll, 1, iCh)) to sCh
            If (bStarted = False) Begin
                // Everything up to the array's own "[" belongs to the key.
                If (sCh = "[") ;
                    Move True to bStarted
            End
            Else If (bInStr = True) Begin
                // Inside a literal every character is content, including a comma or a brace.
                Move (sCur + sCh) to sCur
                If (bEsc = True) ;
                    Move False to bEsc
                Else If (sCh = "\") ;
                    Move True to bEsc
                Else If (sCh = '"') ;
                    Move False to bInStr
            End
            Else If (sCh = " " or sCh = Character(9) or sCh = Character(10)) Begin
                If (bInElem = True) ;
                    Move (sPend + sCh) to sPend
            End
            Else If (sCh = "," and iBrace = 0 and iBrack = 0) Begin
                If (bInElem = True) ;
                    Move sCur to asElem[SizeOfArray(asElem)]
                Move "" to sCur
                Move "" to sPend
                Move False to bInElem
            End
            Else If (sCh = "]" and iBrace = 0 and iBrack = 0) Begin
                // The array's OWN closer: stop, and let the flush below take the last element.
                Move iLen to iCh
            End
            Else Begin
                If (sCh = '"') ;
                    Move True to bInStr
                If (sCh = "{") ;
                    Increment iBrace
                If (sCh = "}") ;
                    Move (iBrace - 1) to iBrace
                If (sCh = "[") ;
                    Increment iBrack
                If (sCh = "]") ;
                    Move (iBrack - 1) to iBrack
                Move (sCur + sPend + sCh) to sCur
                Move "" to sPend
                Move True to bInElem
            End
        Loop
        If (bInElem = True) ;
            Move sCur to asElem[SizeOfArray(asElem)]
        Function_Return asElem
    End_Function

    // Split on Character(10). Deliberately not a general-purpose splitter: the pieces are written
    // straight back to the file, so nothing may be trimmed, folded or re-spaced on the way through.
    Function _SplitOnLf String sText Returns String[]
        String[] asOut
        Integer iPos
        Move (Pos(Character(10), sText)) to iPos
        While (iPos > 0)
            Move (Left(sText, (iPos - 1))) to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
            Move (Right(sText, (Length(sText) - iPos))) to sText
            Move (Pos(Character(10), sText)) to iPos
        Loop
        Move sText to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
        Function_Return asOut
    End_Function

    /* Repoint ONE git dependency at a different commit, in place.

       Line-level on purpose, exactly like WriteSwsLibraryEntries above: the whole file goes back
       out unchanged except the one value, so key order, indentation and every property this reader
       does not model survive untouched. Rebuilding the JSON from a parsed view would drop whatever
       the parse does not carry - the mistake [[feedback_writer_must_not_rebuild_from_a_partial_view]]
       exists to prevent.

       WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL: `df-cli package upgrade` cannot move a git dependency. Measured
       2026-08-21 - asked to upgrade one pinned to an exact SHA it answers "Library <name> is up to
       date." and exits 0, because the pin IS the requested version and there is no range for it to
       resolve. Upgrading a git package therefore means rewriting the pin ourselves and then letting
       df-cli reconcile the installed set to it.

       Matches on the OLD 40-character SHA, which is unique in a .sws, so no dependency other than
       the intended one can be touched. Returns False when the old value is not present. */
    /* EVERY package the workspace ends up with - the ones it names, and the ones THOSE pull in.

       A workspace file records only what the user asked for; whatever those dependencies require is
       installed but never written there. Measured on E:\OrderEntry 2026-08-21: asking for DUF alone
       produced FOUR checkouts under DfPkg - DUF, RDCToolsLib, DFAbout and vWin32fh - against a
       single line in the .sws.

       ONE HOME FOR THE WALK, because two callers need it and they must not disagree: the Libraries
       dialog lists these as rows, and the dashboard tile counts them. The tile has been wrong this
       way before - it once said "1" over a workspace carrying five libraries, "a number that matched
       no other count on screen, least of all the Libraries dialog's own list" - and a second copy of
       this loop is how that happens again.

       A WORK LIST, not recursion: the graph is deeper than one level (vWin32fh arrives under
       RDCToolsLib, which arrives under DUF). Each entry is expanded in turn and anything new is
       appended, so the walk reaches whatever depth exists. Bounded at CI_PackageGraphMaxWalk
       expansions - two manifests naming each other would otherwise spin here.

       Identity for de-duplication is the SOURCE URL, lower-cased: a package reached twice by
       different routes is one package, and the URL is the only field that is certainly the same both
       times (the .sws sub-path and the pinned commit need not be).

       aOut carries the dependencies in walk order, DIRECT ONES FIRST; asVia[i] is the repo that
       required entry i, "" for a direct one; aiDepth[i] is 0 for direct, 1 for its dependency, and
       so on. Returns SizeOfArray(aOut). */
    Function ExpandPackageGraph String sSwsFile String sHomeFolder tSwsDependency[] ByRef aOut ;
                                String[] ByRef asVia Integer[] ByRef aiDepth Returns Integer
        tSwsDependency[] aDirect aNested aEmpty
        String[] asSeen asViaOut asEmptyS
        Integer[] aiDepthOut aiEmptyI
        String sManifest sVia sSrc
        Integer i iMax iAt iGuard iFound

        Move aEmpty   to aOut
        Move asEmptyS to asVia
        Move aiEmptyI to aiDepth
        If (sSwsFile = "") ;
            Function_Return 0

        Get SwsJsonObjectDependencies sSwsFile to aDirect
        Move (SizeOfArray(aDirect) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move aDirect[i] to aOut[SizeOfArray(aOut)]
            Move ""         to asViaOut[SizeOfArray(asViaOut)]
            Move 0          to aiDepthOut[SizeOfArray(aiDepthOut)]
            Move (Lowercase(Trim(aDirect[i].sSource))) to asSeen[SizeOfArray(asSeen)]
        Loop

        Move 0 to iAt
        Move 0 to iGuard
        While (iAt < SizeOfArray(aOut) and iGuard < CI_PackageGraphMaxWalk)
            Increment iGuard
            Get ObjectDependencyManifest sHomeFolder aOut[iAt] to sManifest
            If (sManifest <> "") Begin
                Get GitRepoFromSource (Trim(aOut[iAt].sSource)) to sVia
                Get SwsJsonObjectDependencies sManifest to aNested
                Move (SizeOfArray(aNested) - 1) to iMax
                For i from 0 to iMax
                    Move (Lowercase(Trim(aNested[i].sSource))) to sSrc
                    Move (SearchArray(sSrc, asSeen)) to iFound
                    If (iFound = -1) Begin
                        Move sSrc       to asSeen[SizeOfArray(asSeen)]
                        Move aNested[i] to aOut[SizeOfArray(aOut)]
                        Move sVia       to asViaOut[SizeOfArray(asViaOut)]
                        Move (aiDepthOut[iAt] + 1) to aiDepthOut[SizeOfArray(aiDepthOut)]
                    End
                Loop
            End
            Increment iAt
        Loop

        Move asViaOut   to asVia
        Move aiDepthOut to aiDepth
        Function_Return (SizeOfArray(aOut))
    End_Function

    /*  MOVE ONE GIT DEPENDENCY'S PIN - found by WHO it is, never by its value alone (2026-09-25).

        Until now this replaced the old value on every line that carried it. That was only safe
        while every pin was a 40-character commit, which occurs nowhere else in a workspace file. A
        version TAG is not unique at all: two packages can both be at "1.0.0", the workspace's own
        package version can be "1.0.0", and a pin "1.0.5" is part of "Web UI FlexTron#1.0.54" -
        moving it to 1.0.6 by text alone would have made that FlexTron 1.0.64 as well.

        So this walks the JSON. It knows which object each "version" belongs to, reads straight
        past the inside of every string (a "{" in "${AppSrc}/..." is not a brace), and changes only
        the "version" of the "dependencies" entry whose "source" is sSource - and whose "sws" is
        sSws, when one is given, since one repository can hold two packages - while it still says
        sOldVersion. Exactly one such entry, or nothing is written and the answer is False.

        BYTES IN, BYTES OUT. The old body went through the line reader and the CP850 line writer,
        which turned an LF file (the Studio writes .sws files that way) into CRLF, took the final
        newline off a file that had one, and would have mangled any non-ASCII character in a
        description. Everything the walk looks for is ASCII, and no byte of a UTF-8 multi-byte
        character is, so it cannot misread one - and every byte outside the one value goes back
        exactly as it came. */
    Function SetSwsDependencyVersion String sSwsFile String sSource String sSws String sOldVersion String sNewVersion Returns Boolean
        UChar[] ucaFile ucaNew ucaOut
        String[] asType asKey asSource asSws asVersion
        Integer[] aiVerAt aiVerLen
        String sStr sWantSource sWantSws sHaveSws
        Integer iSize i j iDepth iByte iStrStart iHits iHitAt iHitLen iOut iNew iChannel
        Boolean bInStr bAfterColon

        Move (Trim(sOldVersion)) to sOldVersion
        Move (Trim(sNewVersion)) to sNewVersion
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sSource))) to sWantSource
        Move (Lowercase(Replaces("\", (Trim(sSws)), "/"))) to sWantSws
        If ((sSwsFile = "") or (sWantSource = "") or (sOldVersion = "") or (sNewVersion = "")) ;
            Function_Return False
        // The new value is written between the old quotes as it stands, so it must not close them.
        If ((Pos('"', sNewVersion) > 0) or (Pos("\", sNewVersion) > 0)) ;
            Function_Return False
        If (vFilePathExists(sSwsFile) = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        If (sOldVersion = sNewVersion) ;
            Function_Return True

        Move (rfFileSize(sSwsFile)) to iSize
        If (iSize <= 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (rfReadFileSliceBytes(sSwsFile, 0, iSize)) to ucaFile
        If (SizeOfArray(ucaFile) <> iSize) ;
            Function_Return False

        // Depth 0 is outside everything; the manifest's own object is 1, "dependencies" 2, an entry 3.
        Move ""    to asType[0]
        Move ""    to asKey[0]
        Move 0     to iDepth
        Move 0     to iHits
        Move -1    to iHitAt
        Move 0     to iHitLen
        Move False to bInStr
        Move False to bAfterColon
        Move 0     to i
        While (i < iSize)
            Move ucaFile[i] to iByte
            If (bInStr = True) Begin
                If (iByte = 92) Begin
                    // A backslash escapes the byte after it, whatever it is - a quote included.
                    Increment i
                End
                Else If (iByte = 34) Begin
                    /* The closing quote: a key, or the value of the key before the colon. Keys and
                       the values worth reading are short and ASCII; a long string (a description)
                       is never one of them and is not copied out. */
                    Move False to bInStr
                    Move "" to sStr
                    If ((i - iStrStart) <= 512) Begin
                        For j from iStrStart to (i - 1)
                            Move (sStr + (Character(ucaFile[j]))) to sStr
                        Loop
                    End
                    If ((iDepth > 0) and (asType[iDepth] = "{")) Begin
                        If (bAfterColon = False) ;
                            Move sStr to asKey[iDepth]
                        Else Begin
                            If (asKey[iDepth] = "source") ;
                                Move sStr to asSource[iDepth]
                            If (asKey[iDepth] = "sws") ;
                                Move sStr to asSws[iDepth]
                            If (asKey[iDepth] = "version") Begin
                                Move sStr            to asVersion[iDepth]
                                Move iStrStart       to aiVerAt[iDepth]
                                Move (i - iStrStart) to aiVerLen[iDepth]
                            End
                            Move False to bAfterColon
                        End
                    End
                End
            End
            Else Begin
                If (iByte = 34) Begin
                    Move True    to bInStr
                    Move (i + 1) to iStrStart
                End
                // { and [ open a level, and its slots start empty.
                If ((iByte = 123) or (iByte = 91)) Begin
                    Increment iDepth
                    Move (If((iByte = 123), "{", "[")) to asType[iDepth]
                    Move "" to asKey[iDepth]
                    Move "" to asSource[iDepth]
                    Move "" to asSws[iDepth]
                    Move "" to asVersion[iDepth]
                    Move -1 to aiVerAt[iDepth]
                    Move 0  to aiVerLen[iDepth]
                    Move False to bAfterColon
                End
                /* } and ] close one. The match is decided as an entry closes: an object, inside an
                   array, which is the value of "dependencies" one level further up. */
                If ((iByte = 125) or (iByte = 93)) Begin
                    If ((iByte = 125) and (iDepth >= 3)) Begin
                        If ((asType[iDepth] = "{") and (asType[iDepth - 1] = "[") and (asKey[iDepth - 2] = "dependencies")) Begin
                            Move (Lowercase(Replaces("\", (Trim(asSws[iDepth])), "/"))) to sHaveSws
                            If ((Lowercase(Trim(asSource[iDepth])) = sWantSource) and ((sWantSws = "") or (sHaveSws = sWantSws)) ;
                                and (Trim(asVersion[iDepth]) = sOldVersion) and (aiVerAt[iDepth] >= 0)) Begin
                                Increment iHits
                                Move aiVerAt[iDepth]  to iHitAt
                                Move aiVerLen[iDepth] to iHitLen
                            End
                        End
                    End
                    If (iDepth > 0) ;
                        Decrement iDepth
                    Move False to bAfterColon
                End
                If (iByte = 58) ;
                    Move True to bAfterColon
                If (iByte = 44) ;
                    Move False to bAfterColon
            End
            Increment i
        Loop
        If (iHits <> 1) ;
            Function_Return False

        // Everything before the value, the new value, everything after it - byte for byte.
        Move (rfStringToUtf8Bytes(sNewVersion)) to ucaNew
        Move (SizeOfArray(ucaNew)) to iNew
        Move 0 to iOut
        For j from 0 to (iHitAt - 1)
            Move ucaFile[j] to ucaOut[iOut]
            Increment iOut
        Loop
        For j from 0 to (iNew - 1)
            Move ucaNew[j] to ucaOut[iOut]
            Increment iOut
        Loop
        For j from (iHitAt + iHitLen) to (iSize - 1)
            Move ucaFile[j] to ucaOut[iOut]
            Increment iOut
        Loop

        Move (Seq_New_Channel()) to iChannel
        If (iChannel < 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Direct_Output Channel iChannel ("binary:" + sSwsFile)
        // A file that cannot be opened (read-only, locked) raises nothing here - only SeqEof says so.
        If (SeqEof) Begin
            Close_Output Channel iChannel
            Send Seq_Release_Channel iChannel
            Function_Return False
        End
        Write Channel iChannel ucaOut
        Close_Output Channel iChannel
        Send Seq_Release_Channel iChannel
        Function_Return (Err = False)
    End_Function

    /* A JSON file's text, decoded as the UTF-8 it is (a .sws and its .lock carry no BOM) - "" when
       it is missing or empty. */
    Function _JsonFileText String sFile Returns String
        UChar[] ucaFile
        Integer iSize

        If (vFilePathExists(sFile) = 0) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (rfFileSize(sFile)) to iSize
        If (iSize <= 0) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (rfReadFileSliceBytes(sFile, 0, iSize)) to ucaFile
        Move (rfStripUtf8Bom(ucaFile)) to ucaFile
        Function_Return (rfUtf8BytesToString(ucaFile))
    End_Function

    /* ucaBytes to sFile as they are - a binary channel, nothing translated. False when the file
       cannot be opened (read-only, locked): that raises nothing, only SeqEof says so. */
    Function _WriteFileBytes String sFile UChar[] ucaBytes Returns Boolean
        Integer iChannel

        Move (Seq_New_Channel()) to iChannel
        If (iChannel < 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Direct_Output Channel iChannel ("binary:" + sFile)
        If (SeqEof) Begin
            Close_Output Channel iChannel
            Send Seq_Release_Channel iChannel
            Function_Return False
        End
        Write Channel iChannel ucaBytes
        Close_Output Channel iChannel
        Send Seq_Release_Channel iChannel
        Function_Return (Err = False)
    End_Function

    /* THE LOCK FILE AS THE CAUSE (Nils 2026-10-29, LegacyApp5_20). df-cli refused VDFQuery20 with
       "Incompatible ref for NilsSve/vWin32fh-Library-DF26.0: 1.0.0 does not match 5432741c...". No
       dependency of the workspace asked for that commit any more - DUF and RDCToolsLib had both
       moved to ^1.0.0 - only LegacyApp5_20.sws.lock, which had kept it since an earlier DUF pin. Drop
       that one entry and df-cli chooses the version again.

       Answers the ref the lock pins the package at when ALL of these hold, else "":
         - asCliLines carries df-cli's incompatible-ref line, ONE lock entry names that package,
           and it pins it at one of the line's two refs;
         - neither the workspace's own .sws nor the .sws of any OTHER git package the lock pins
           asks for the package at that ref. Each is read from its DfPkg checkout - the folder
           ending in "-<ref>" that holds its .sws (the key's tail after ".git/"). One that cannot be
           found answers "": an ask that cannot be read is not an ask that is gone.
       Registry entries ("DataFlex-dev/Web UI") are passed over - they live in the package cache,
       not under DfPkg, and do not ask for a git commit. sPkgName gets df-cli's name for the
       package, sLockKey the entry's key, for RemoveLockEntry. */
    Function StaleLockPin String sSwsFile String[] asCliLines String ByRef sPkgName String ByRef sLockKey Returns String
        tRFSearchResult[] aDirs
        String[] asKeys asRefs
        String sPkg sA sB sLockRef sText sDfPkg sRel sDir sCandidate
        Integer i iMax iTarget iPos d
        Boolean bFound bDirsRead bRead

        Move "" to sPkgName
        Move "" to sLockKey
        If (sSwsFile = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Get DfCliIncompatibleRef (&asCliLines) (&sPkg) (&sA) (&sB) to bFound
        If (bFound = False) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Get _JsonFileText (sSwsFile + ".lock") to sText
        Get LockFileEntries sText (&asKeys) (&asRefs) to iMax
        Move -1 to iTarget
        For i from 0 to (iMax - 1)
            If (LockKeyNamesPackage(asKeys[i], sPkg) = True) Begin
                // Two entries for one name: not this function's to choose between.
                If (iTarget <> -1) ;
                    Function_Return ""
                Move i to iTarget
            End
        Loop
        If (iTarget = -1) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move asRefs[iTarget] to sLockRef
        If ((sLockRef = "") or ((Lowercase(sLockRef) <> Lowercase(sA)) and (Lowercase(sLockRef) <> Lowercase(sB)))) ;
            Function_Return ""

        // The workspace's own ask.
        Get _JsonFileText sSwsFile to sText
        If (SwsJsonAsksForRef(sText, sPkg, sLockRef) = True) ;
            Function_Return ""

        // Every other git package the lock pins, read from its checkout.
        Move (Replaces("/", sSwsFile, "\")) to sDfPkg
        Move (RightPos("\", sDfPkg)) to iPos
        If (iPos = 0) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (Left(sDfPkg, iPos) + "DfPkg") to sDfPkg
        Move False to bDirsRead
        For i from 0 to (iMax - 1)
            Move (Pos(".git/", Lowercase(asKeys[i]))) to iPos
            If ((i <> iTarget) and (iPos > 0)) Begin
                If (asRefs[i] = "") ;
                    Function_Return ""
                If (bDirsRead = False) Begin
                    If (rfDirectoryExists(sDfPkg) = False) ;
                        Function_Return ""
                    Move (rfFileSearch((sDfPkg + "\*"), DIRMODE_DIRECTORIES_ONLY, False)) to aDirs
                    Move True to bDirsRead
                End
                // ".git/" is 5 characters; what follows it is the .sws path inside the checkout.
                Move (Replaces("/", (Right(asKeys[i], Length(asKeys[i]) - iPos - 4) + ".sws"), "\")) to sRel
                Move False to bRead
                For d from 0 to (SizeOfArray(aDirs) - 1)
                    Move (Trim(aDirs[d].sFilename)) to sDir
                    If ((bRead = False) and (Lowercase(Right(sDir, Length(asRefs[i]) + 1)) = ("-" + Lowercase(asRefs[i])))) Begin
                        Move (sDfPkg + "\" + sDir + "\" + sRel) to sCandidate
                        If (vFilePathExists(sCandidate) <> 0) Begin
                            Get _JsonFileText sCandidate to sText
                            Move True to bRead
                        End
                    End
                Loop
                If (bRead = False) ;
                    Function_Return ""
                If (SwsJsonAsksForRef(sText, sPkg, sLockRef) = True) ;
                    Function_Return ""
            End
        Loop
        Move sPkg to sPkgName
        Move asKeys[iTarget] to sLockKey
        Function_Return sLockRef
    End_Function

    /* Remove ONE entry - the one StaleLockPin named - from a .sws.lock, and hand back the file's
       bytes as they were, so a retry that still fails can put it back exactly (RestoreFileBytes).
       Re-written through cJsonObject, pretty-printed: df-cli rewrites the lock on its next run
       anyway, and a failed retry restores the original bytes. False when the entry is not there or
       the file cannot be written - then nothing has changed. */
    Function RemoveLockEntry String sLockFile String sLockKey UChar[] ByRef ucaBefore Returns Boolean
        Handle hoJson hoDeps
        UChar[] ucaNone ucaNew
        Integer iSize
        Boolean bOk bHas

        Move ucaNone to ucaBefore
        If ((Trim(sLockKey) = "") or (vFilePathExists(sLockFile) = 0)) ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (rfFileSize(sLockFile)) to iSize
        If (iSize <= 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (rfReadFileSliceBytes(sLockFile, 0, iSize)) to ucaBefore
        If (SizeOfArray(ucaBefore) <> iSize) Begin
            Move ucaNone to ucaBefore
            Function_Return False
        End
        Move False to bHas
        Get Create (RefClass(cJsonObject)) to hoJson
        Get ParseUtf8 of hoJson (rfStripUtf8Bom(ucaBefore)) to bOk
        If (bOk = True) Begin
            Set pbRequireAllMembers of hoJson to False
            Get MemberEnh of hoJson "dependencies" to hoDeps
            If (hoDeps <> 0) Begin
                Get HasMember of hoDeps sLockKey to bHas
                If (bHas = True) Begin
                    Send RemoveMember of hoDeps sLockKey
                    // Set back explicitly, whether Member handed out a copy or a reference.
                    Send SetMember of hoJson "dependencies" hoDeps
                    Set peWhiteSpace of hoJson to jpWhitespace_Pretty
                    Get StringifyUtf8 of hoJson to ucaNew
                End
                Send Destroy of hoDeps
            End
        End
        Send Destroy of hoJson
        If ((bHas = False) or (SizeOfArray(ucaNew) = 0)) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get _WriteFileBytes sLockFile ucaNew to bOk
        Function_Return bOk
    End_Function

    // The other half of RemoveLockEntry: the file back exactly as it was read.
    Function RestoreFileBytes String sFile UChar[] ucaBytes Returns Boolean
        Boolean bOk

        If (SizeOfArray(ucaBytes) = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get _WriteFileBytes sFile ucaBytes to bOk
        Function_Return bOk
    End_Function

    Function WriteSwsLibraryEntries String sSwsFile String[] asEntries Returns Boolean
        String[] asLines asOut
        String sLine sTrim sEntry
        Integer i iMax iFormat iN iOut
        Boolean bInLibs bEmitted bDrop bOk

        If (vFilePathExists(sSwsFile) = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get SwsFormatOf sSwsFile to iFormat
        If (iFormat = eSwsJson) Begin
            Get _WriteSwsJsonDependencies sSwsFile asEntries to bOk
            Function_Return bOk
        End

        Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sSwsFile)) to asLines
        If (SizeOfArray(asLines) = 0) ;
            Function_Return False

        Move (SizeOfArray(asEntries) - 1) to iN
        Move False to bInLibs
        Move False to bEmitted

        Move (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move asLines[i] to sLine
            Move (Trim(Replaces(Character(9), (Replaces(Character(13), sLine, "")), " "))) to sTrim
            // An existing entry inside [Libraries] is DROPPED - asEntries replaces the whole set.
            // Comments and blank lines in there are not entries and are kept.
            Move False to bDrop
            If (bInLibs = True and Left(sTrim, 1) <> "[" and Left(sTrim, 1) <> ";" and Left(sTrim, 2) <> "//") ;
                Move (Pos("=", sTrim) > 1) to bDrop
            If (Left(sTrim, 1) = "[") Begin
                // The section is ENDING (or another one is opening): our block goes in before the
                // next header, so the entries land inside [Libraries] and not under its successor.
                If (bInLibs = True and bEmitted = False) Begin
                    For iOut from 0 to iN
                        Move (Trim(asEntries[iOut])) to sEntry
                        If (sEntry <> "") ;
                            Move ("Lib" + String(iOut + 1) + "=" + sEntry) to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                    Loop
                    Move True to bEmitted
                End
                Move (Lowercase(sTrim) = "[libraries]") to bInLibs
            End
            If (bDrop = False) ;
                Move sLine to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
        Loop

        // The section ran to end-of-file, or there was no [Libraries] header at all.
        If (bEmitted = False) Begin
            If (bInLibs = False and iN >= 0) ;
                Move "[Libraries]" to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
            If (bInLibs = True or iN >= 0) Begin
                For iOut from 0 to iN
                    Move (Trim(asEntries[iOut])) to sEntry
                    If (sEntry <> "") ;
                        Move ("Lib" + String(iOut + 1) + "=" + sEntry) to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                Loop
            End
        End

        Get rfWriteEncodedFileFromStringArray (&asOut) sSwsFile C_NewLine False to bOk
        Function_Return bOk
    End_Function

    /* ---- DF26 DEPENDENCY CLASSIFICATION --------------------------------------------------------
       Pure string work, no disk, so the whole set is testable from the console runner. */

    /* WHAT KIND OF ENTRY IS THIS? Under DF26 a dependency string is either a DataFlex system package or a
       path, and telling them apart has to happen BEFORE anything tries to resolve one.

       The object form (eDepGit) never reaches here - it is not a string, and the reader decides it.

       Ordered so that every POSITIVE path signal is tested first and the package answer is what is
       left over. That is the safe direction: a misread package renders as an odd-looking row, while
       a misread path silently stops resolving a real library.
         .sws suffix     - decisive. A package string names no file.
         absolute        - a drive or UNC.
         leading "."     - ".\", "..\", "./", "../"
         any backslash   - no package string contains one; every Windows path does.
       What remains, and carries a "<repo>/<Name>" slash, is a package. Anything else falls through
       to eDepPath, which is exactly the pre-DF26 behaviour. */
    Function DependencyKind String sEntry Returns Integer
        String sLow

        Move (Trim(sEntry)) to sEntry
        If (sEntry = "") ;
            Function_Return eDepPath
        Move (Lowercase(sEntry)) to sLow
        If (Right(sLow, 4) = ".sws") ;
            Function_Return eDepPath
        If (_IsAbsolutePath(Self, sEntry)) ;
            Function_Return eDepPath
        If (Left(sEntry, 1) = ".") ;
            Function_Return eDepPath
        If (Pos("\", sEntry) > 0) ;
            Function_Return eDepPath
        If (Pos("/", sEntry) > 1) ;
            Function_Return eDepPackage
        Function_Return eDepPath
    End_Function

    // The repository half of "<repo>/<Name>#<version>" - "DataFlex-dev". "" if there is none.
    Function PackageRepository String sEntry Returns String
        Integer iPos
        Move (Trim(sEntry)) to sEntry
        Move (Pos("/", sEntry)) to iPos
        If (iPos < 2) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Function_Return (Left(sEntry, iPos - 1))
    End_Function

    /* The package NAME - "Web UI FlexTron". Spaces are ordinary in these names, which is why nothing
       here splits on whitespace. Falls back to the whole entry so a row is never nameless. */
    Function PackageName String sEntry Returns String
        String sRest
        Integer iPos
        Move (Trim(sEntry)) to sEntry
        Move (Pos("/", sEntry)) to iPos
        If (iPos < 1) ;
            Move sEntry to sRest
        Else ;
            Move (Mid(sEntry, (Length(sEntry) - iPos), (iPos + 1))) to sRest
        Move (Pos("#", sRest)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 1) ;
            Move (Left(sRest, iPos - 1)) to sRest
        Function_Return (Trim(sRest))
    End_Function

    // The PINNED version after "#" - "1.0.51", or "" when the entry names no version. Unlike a
    // library's version this is declared by the CONSUMER, so it is what the workspace asked for.
    Function PackageVersion String sEntry Returns String
        Integer iPos
        Move (Trim(sEntry)) to sEntry
        Move (Pos("#", sEntry)) to iPos
        If (iPos < 1) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Function_Return (Trim(Mid(sEntry, (Length(sEntry) - iPos), (iPos + 1))))
    End_Function

    /* Where df-cli puts a package on disk. Packages live in a GLOBAL cache, not in the workspace:

           %ProgramData%\DataFlex\Packages\Cache\<Repo>_<Name>-<version>\

       with "-" and " " folded to "_" in the repo and name halves, verified against real entries
       ("DataFlex-dev/Web UI FlexTron#1.0.44" -> "DataFlex_dev_Web_UI_FlexTron-1.0.44"). Returns ""
       for an entry with no pinned version, because the folder name cannot then be derived - the row
       says "no version pinned" rather than guessing at one. */
    /* The root of df-cli's global package cache, WITH its trailing separator.

       %ProgramData%\DataFlex\Packages\Cache\ - verified on this machine, and where df-cli's own
       "cache" command reports its entries.

       WRITTEN OUT rather than asked of the shell. The obvious call, vSHGetFolderPath with
       vCSIDL_COMMON_APPDATA, is NOT a global function in vwin32fh - so the expression form
       vSHGetFolderPath(vCSIDL_COMMON_APPDATA) parses as "send to the object whose id is 0x0023"
       and dies with error 57, receiver Status_Panel. (0x0023 is 35, and object 35 was the status
       panel: the constant became the receiver and the argument vanished.) Getting that call right
       needs a receiver this class has no business knowing, and it cannot be exercised from the
       console runner where the rest of this file is tested.

       The cost of the assumption is bounded and visible: %ProgramData% is relocatable in theory and
       is C:\ProgramData on a standard install, and a wrong root does not break anything - the
       package row simply reports "not in the package cache", which is also what it says when a
       package genuinely is not installed. A crash was the worse trade.

       A separate function so a host that knows better can override it in a subclass, the same
       escape hatch the JSON hooks use. */
    Function PackageCacheRoot Returns String
        Function_Return "C:\ProgramData\DataFlex\Packages\Cache\"
    End_Function

    Function PackageCacheFolder String sEntry Returns String
        String sRepo sName sVer sRoot

        Get PackageRepository sEntry to sRepo
        Get PackageName sEntry to sName
        Get PackageVersion sEntry to sVer
        If (sName = "" or sVer = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (Replaces("-", sRepo, "_")) to sRepo
        Move (Replaces(" ", sRepo, "_")) to sRepo
        Move (Replaces("-", sName, "_")) to sName
        Move (Replaces(" ", sName, "_")) to sName
        Get PackageCacheRoot to sRoot
        If (sRoot = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        If (sRepo <> "") ;
            Function_Return (sRoot + sRepo + "_" + sName + "-" + sVer + "\")
        Function_Return (sRoot + sName + "-" + sVer + "\")
    End_Function

    /* WHAT A PACKAGE SAYS ABOUT ITSELF (2026-08-19). An installed package carries a df.sws in its
       cache folder, and that file - not the workspace's dependency array - is where its human-facing
       metadata lives:

           { "description": "Conversion library to convert values from one format to the other.",
             "df": 26.0,
             "package": { "df-max": 26.0, "df-min": 26.0, "icon": "Icon.png", "version": "1.0.0" },
             "workspaceName": "Conversions" }

       This is the same information the Studio shows and that "df-cli package search" returns in its
       Description column - so a card can describe a package without spawning df-cli per row.

       Read as TEXT rather than through ghoApplication's JSON reader on purpose: that reader returns a
       tSwsFile built for dependencies and paths, extending it would touch every caller, and this class
       is deliberately free of an application reference. df-cli writes the file one key per line, so a
       per-line scan is enough - and a key that is absent (every INI-era package, and any repo that has
       not declared one) simply returns "", which the caller renders as nothing.

       sKey is matched WITH its quotes, so "version" cannot match "df-version"; nested keys such as the
       package object's "icon" are found the same way, since the scan does not care about depth. */
    /* The scalar reader itself, on ANY JSON .sws - a cached package's df.sws, or a library's own
       workspace file once it has been converted. Split out from PackageManifestValue on 2026-08-19:
       the cache is only where a SYSTEM package keeps its manifest, and a library referenced by path
       never goes near it - so reading only there meant a library could declare a description and the
       card would still show none. */
    /* How much this line OPENS or CLOSES, ignoring braces inside a JSON string. It has to ignore them:
       a real manifest carries "${AppSrc}/FlexTronScriptIncludes.html" as a value, and counting that
       value's braces would put the scanner permanently out of step with the file. */
    Function _JsonBraceDelta String sLine Returns Integer
        Integer i iLen iDelta
        Boolean bInStr bEsc
        String sCh

        Move (Length(sLine)) to iLen
        Move 0 to iDelta
        Move False to bInStr
        Move False to bEsc
        For i from 1 to iLen
            Move (Mid(sLine, 1, i)) to sCh
            If (bEsc = True) Begin
                Move False to bEsc
            End
            Else Begin
                If ((sCh = "\") and (bInStr = True)) Begin
                    Move True to bEsc
                End
                Else Begin
                    If (sCh = '"') Begin
                        If (bInStr = True) Begin
                            Move False to bInStr
                        End
                        Else Begin
                            Move True to bInStr
                        End
                    End
                    Else Begin
                        If (bInStr = False) Begin
                            If ((sCh = "{") or (sCh = "[")) ;
                                Move (iDelta + 1) to iDelta
                            If ((sCh = "}") or (sCh = "]")) ;
                                Move (iDelta - 1) to iDelta
                        End
                    End
                End
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return iDelta
    End_Function

    // The quoted name a line starts with - its key, or "" for a line that carries none.
    Function _JsonLineKey String sLine Returns String
        Integer iOpen iClose
        Move (Pos('"', sLine)) to iOpen
        If (iOpen = 0) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (Pos('"', sLine, (iOpen + 1))) to iClose
        If (iClose <= iOpen) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Function_Return (Mid(sLine, (iClose - iOpen - 1), (iOpen + 1)))
    End_Function

    Function SwsManifestValue String sSwsFile String sKey Returns String
        String[] asLines
        String sLine sVal sQuoted sLineKey sSection
        Integer i iMax iPos iOpen iClose iDepth

        If (Trim(sSwsFile) = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        If (vFilePathExists(sSwsFile) = 0) ;
            Function_Return ""
        If (SwsFormatOf(Self, sSwsFile) <> eSwsJson) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sSwsFile)) to asLines
        Move ('"' + Trim(sKey) + '"') to sQuoted
        Move (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1) to iMax
        Move 0 to iDepth
        Move "" to sSection
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move asLines[i] to sLine
            Get _JsonLineKey sLine to sLineKey
            /* SCOPE, NOT JUST A NAME MATCH (2026-08-19). The first cut took the key from anywhere in
               the file and said so proudly - "the scan does not care about depth". For "description"
               that is harmless; for "version" it is a trap, because a workspace's dependency records
               each carry a "version" of their own holding a 40-character commit. A library that
               gained one git dependency would have shown THAT dependency's hash as its own version,
               confidently and wrongly. So a key counts only where df-cli puts a package's own
               scalars: at the manifest's top level, or inside its "package" object - which is where
               DataFlex's own packages keep both "version" and "icon". Anything deeper, and
               "dependencies" above all, is somebody else's answer. */
            Move (Pos(sQuoted, sLine)) to iPos
            If ((iPos > 0) and (sLineKey = (Trim(sKey))) ;
                    and ((iDepth = 1) or ((iDepth = 2) and (sSection = "package")))) Begin
                // Past the key and its colon, the value is the next quoted run on the line.
                Move (Pos(":", sLine, (iPos + Length(sQuoted)))) to iPos
                If (iPos > 0) Begin
                    Move (Pos('"', sLine, (iPos + 1))) to iOpen
                    If (iOpen > 0) Begin
                        Move (Pos('"', sLine, (iOpen + 1))) to iClose
                        If (iClose > iOpen) Begin
                            Move (Mid(sLine, (iClose - iOpen - 1), (iOpen + 1))) to sVal
                            Function_Return sVal
                        End
                    End
                    /* AN UNQUOTED SCALAR still counts. JSON numbers and booleans carry no quotes,
                       and two of the keys worth reading are numbers: "df-min": 26.0 and
                       "df-max": 26.0. Before this the quoted branch simply fell through and the
                       caller got "" - indistinguishable from "the key is absent", which is a
                       different fact. Everything after the colon, minus a trailing comma. */
                    Move (Trim(Right(sLine, (Length(sLine) - iPos)))) to sVal
                    If (Right(sVal, 1) = ",") ;
                        Move (Trim(Left(sVal, (Length(sVal) - 1)))) to sVal
                    Function_Return sVal
                End
            End
            /* The section this line NAMES, captured while the scanner is still at the top level: the
               line that opens "package" is read at depth 1, and only afterwards does depth become 2. */
            If ((iDepth = 1) and (sLineKey <> "")) ;
                Move sLineKey to sSection
            Move (iDepth + (_JsonBraceDelta(Self, sLine))) to iDepth
            If (iDepth < 2) ;
                Move "" to sSection
        Loop
        Function_Return ""
    End_Function

    /* A GIT DEPENDENCY'S OWN .sws, inside the workspace's DfPkg checkout - or "" when it is not
       checked out (2026-08-19). This is the third place a manifest can live, after a library's own
       file and a DataFlex system package's cache entry, and without it a git dependency was the one kind of
       row that could describe itself and did not: Nils published a JSON .sws to his repo, df-cli
       checked it out, and the card still showed the FILE STEM as a title and the 40-character commit
       as a version.

       The folder is FOUND, not derived. df-cli names it "<Owner>_<sws stem>-<version>" with hyphens
       and spaces folded to underscores - NilsSve_DFAbout_Library_DF26-8b5158ab... - and rebuilding
       that string here would be one more place to disagree with df-cli about a name it owns (the
       standing rule about library filenames, one level down). Instead the checkout that ENDS with
       this dependency's version and carries its .sws is the one, which stays right however df-cli
       decides to spell the rest. */
    Function ObjectDependencyManifest String sHomeFolder tSwsDependency Dep Returns String
        tRFSearchResult[] aDirs
        String sDfPkg sVer sSws sCandidate sDir sOnly
        Integer i iMax iCarriers

        Move (Trim(Dep.sVersion)) to sVer
        Move (Trim(Dep.sSws)) to sSws
        If (sHomeFolder = "" or sVer = "" or sSws = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (Replaces("/", sSws, "\")) to sSws
        Move (vFolderFormat(sHomeFolder) + "DfPkg") to sDfPkg
        If (rfDirectoryExists(sDfPkg) = False) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (rfFileSearch((vFolderFormat(sDfPkg) + "*"), DIRMODE_DIRECTORIES_ONLY, False)) to aDirs
        Move (SizeOfArray(aDirs) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (Trim(aDirs[i].sFilename)) to sDir
            If (Right(sDir, (Length(sVer) + 1)) = ("-" + sVer)) Begin
                Move (vFolderFormat(vFolderFormat(sDfPkg) + sDir) + sSws) to sCandidate
                If (vFilePathExists(sCandidate) <> 0) ;
                    Function_Return sCandidate
            End
        Loop

        /* A RANGE NAMES NO FOLDER (2026-09-25). A library that asks for "^1.0.0" gets whichever tag
           df-cli resolved, and the checkout is named after THAT tag ("...-1.0.1"), so the suffix
           test above can never match it. The one checkout that carries this dependency's .sws is
           then the answer - but only when it IS one: df-cli leaves the previous version's folder
           behind after an update, and with two to choose from this answers "" rather than guess.
           A commit or an exact tag DOES name its folder, so for those a miss above stays a miss. */
        If ((IsGitCommitPin(sVer) = True) or (IsVersionTagPin(sVer) = True)) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move "" to sOnly
        Move 0  to iCarriers
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (vFolderFormat(vFolderFormat(sDfPkg) + (Trim(aDirs[i].sFilename))) + sSws) to sCandidate
            If (vFilePathExists(sCandidate) <> 0) Begin
                Move sCandidate to sOnly
                Increment iCarriers
            End
        Loop
        If (iCarriers = 1) ;
            Function_Return sOnly
        Function_Return ""
    End_Function

    /* WHO AND WHAT, read off the git URL (Nils 2026-08-19). A git dependency's identity is the repo it
       came from - "https://github.com/NilsSve/Library-DFAbout.git" is owner NilsSve, repo
       Library-DFAbout - and that is what a reader recognises. Neither the .sws file stem
       ("DFAbout-Library-DF26", which is a build artefact's name) nor the manifest's workspaceName
       ("DFAbout", which is what the workspace calls itself) is the thing you went to GitHub for.

       Trailing ".git" and any trailing slash are dropped; an ssh form (git@github.com:Owner/Repo.git)
       is handled by taking the last two segments after either separator. Anything that does not have
       two segments answers "", and the caller keeps whatever it had. */
    Function GitRepoFromSource String sSource Returns String
        String sS
        Integer iPos
        Move (Trim(sSource)) to sS
        Move (Replaces(":", (Replaces("/", sS, "\")), "\")) to sS
        If (Right(sS, 1) = "\") ;
            Move (Left(sS, (Length(sS) - 1))) to sS
        If (Lowercase(Right(sS, 4)) = ".git") ;
            Move (Left(sS, (Length(sS) - 4))) to sS
        Move (RightPos("\", sS)) to iPos
        If (iPos = 0 or iPos = Length(sS)) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Function_Return (Right(sS, (Length(sS) - iPos)))
    End_Function

    Function GitOwnerFromSource String sSource Returns String
        String sS
        Integer iPos

        Move (Trim(sSource)) to sS
        Move (Replaces(":", (Replaces("/", sS, "\")), "\")) to sS
        If (Right(sS, 1) = "\") ;
            Move (Left(sS, (Length(sS) - 1))) to sS
        If (Lowercase(Right(sS, 4)) = ".git") ;
            Move (Left(sS, (Length(sS) - 4))) to sS
        Move (RightPos("\", sS)) to iPos
        If (iPos < 2) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (Left(sS, (iPos - 1))) to sS
        Move (RightPos("\", sS)) to iPos
        If (iPos = 0 or iPos = Length(sS)) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Function_Return (Right(sS, (Length(sS) - iPos)))
    End_Function

    /* A commit is not a version anybody reads. A 40-character SHA fills the version slot with noise,
       so it is shortened to the 7 characters git itself shows; anything that is not a bare hex SHA
       (a real "1.0.0") is left exactly as declared. */
    Function ShortDependencyVersion String sVersion Returns String
        String sV sCh
        Integer i iLen
        Move (Trim(sVersion)) to sV
        Move (Length(sV)) to iLen
        If (iLen < 20) ;
            Function_Return sV
        For i from 1 to iLen
            Move (Lowercase(Mid(sV, 1, i))) to sCh
            If (Pos(sCh, "0123456789abcdef") = 0) ;
                Function_Return sV
        Loop
        Function_Return (Left(sV, 7))
    End_Function

    // A SYSTEM package's manifest lives in its cache folder, as df.sws.
    Function PackageManifestValue String sEntry String sKey Returns String
        String sFolder
        Get PackageCacheFolder sEntry to sFolder
        If (sFolder = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Function_Return (SwsManifestValue(Self, (sFolder + "df.sws"), sKey))
    End_Function

    // The package's icon as a full path, or "" when it declares none / is not installed. The file
    // lives beside its df.sws in the cache folder.
    Function PackageIconPath String sEntry Returns String
        String sIcon sFolder
        Get PackageManifestValue sEntry "icon" to sIcon
        If (Trim(sIcon) = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Get PackageCacheFolder sEntry to sFolder
        If (sFolder = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (sFolder + Trim(sIcon)) to sIcon
        If (vFilePathExists(sIcon) = 0) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Function_Return sIcon
    End_Function

    /* Joins a workspace-relative [Libraries] path onto the workspace home.

       Delegates to _ResolvePath rather than doing it here, which fixes two defects the hand-rolled
       version had. It stripped a leading ".\" but never collapsed "..\", so a perfectly ordinary
       entry - "Lib1=..\Libraries\vdfquery\VDFQuery182.sws", a library ABOVE the workspace, which is
       how a shared pool is normally referenced - came back as
       "C:\Projects\LegacyApp10\..\Libraries\vdfquery\VDFQuery182.sws". Windows opens that happily, so the
       inventory worked and the flaw travelled downstream into tLibraryInfo.sFolder and on into the
       copy plan's rows (2026-08-06). And it tested absoluteness with (sRawPath contains ":"), which
       misses a UNC entry entirely and would prepend the home to "\\server\share\X.sws".

       _IsAbsolutePath answers both, and NormalizePath makes the result comparable. */
    { Visibility=Private }
    Function ResolveLibraryPath String sRawPath String sHomePath Returns String
        Move (Trim(sRawPath)) to sRawPath
        If (sRawPath = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Function_Return (_ResolvePath(Self, sHomePath, sRawPath))
    End_Function

    /* Is this .sws file name one a CONSUMER may reference? Excludes the development / demo /
       sample roles that sit beside it. Nils's own convention is "RDCToolsLibDev25.0.sws" (for
       developing the library) beside "RDCToolsLibLibrary25.0.sws" (for consuming it), and SigCJ
       ships Classes / Demo / Wrapper - so matching on version alone picks the right version and
       the wrong role. Exclusion, not a required "library" marker: DFAbout25.0.sws and
       DFUnit-25.0.sws carry no role word at all.

       The marker must stand as its own word (bounded by a non-letter or the ends of the stem),
       or a name like "DevExpress" would be excluded wrongly. */
    Function IsConsumableLibrarySws String sFileName Returns Boolean
        String[] asMarkers
        String sStem sLow sCh
        Integer i iMax iPos iAfter
        Boolean bWordStart bWordEnd

        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sFileName))) to sLow
        If (Right(sLow, 4) <> ".sws") ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (Left(sLow, (Length(sLow) - 4))) to sStem
        Move (StrSplitToArray(CS_LibNonConsumableMarkers, "|")) to asMarkers
        Move (SizeOfArray(asMarkers) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (Pos(asMarkers[i], sStem)) to iPos
            If (iPos > 0) Begin
                Move True to bWordStart
                If (iPos > 1) Begin
                    Move (Mid(sStem, 1, (iPos - 1))) to sCh
                    Move (sCh < "a" or sCh > "z") to bWordStart
                End
                Move True to bWordEnd
                Move (iPos + Length(asMarkers[i])) to iAfter
                If (iAfter <= Length(sStem)) Begin
                    Move (Mid(sStem, 1, iAfter)) to sCh
                    Move (sCh < "a" or sCh > "z") to bWordEnd
                End
                If (bWordStart and bWordEnd) ;
                    Function_Return False
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    /* A library's display name: the folder holding its .sws, but skipping generic container
       folders. vwin32fh's .sws lives in "vwin32fh\StudioLibrary\", so the leaf alone would call
       every such library "StudioLibrary" - which is exactly what the raw folder count did to 293
       references in the survey. Walks up at most two levels looking for a non-generic name. */
    Function LibraryDisplayName String sFolder Returns String
        String sRoot sLeaf
        Integer iPos
        Get LibraryRootFolder sFolder to sRoot
        If (Right(sRoot, 1) = "\" or Right(sRoot, 1) = "/") ;
            Move (Left(sRoot, (Length(sRoot) - 1))) to sRoot
        Move (RightPos("\", sRoot)) to iPos
        If (iPos = 0) ;
            Move (RightPos("/", sRoot)) to iPos
        If (iPos = 0) ;
            Function_Return sRoot
        Move (Mid(sRoot, (Length(sRoot) - iPos), (iPos + 1))) to sLeaf
        Function_Return sLeaf
    End_Function

    /* The library's ROOT folder - the path whose leaf LibraryDisplayName reports, so the two can
       never disagree.

       Not the folder holding the .sws. vwin32fh's .sws lives in "vwin32fh\StudioLibrary\" while its
       packages (vWin32fh.pkg, cvFileDialogs.pkg) sit one level UP in "vwin32fh\" - so a path test
       anchored on the .sws folder would miss the library's actual source completely. Walks up past
       the generic container names for exactly that reason. */
    Function LibraryRootFolder String sFolder Returns String
        String[] asGeneric
        String sPath sLeaf
        Integer iHop iPos

        Move sFolder to sPath
        Move (StrSplitToArray(CS_LibGenericFolders, "|")) to asGeneric
        For iHop from 0 to 2
            If (Right(sPath, 1) = "\" or Right(sPath, 1) = "/") ;
                Move (Left(sPath, (Length(sPath) - 1))) to sPath
            Move (RightPos("\", sPath)) to iPos
            If (iPos = 0) ;
                Move (RightPos("/", sPath)) to iPos
            If (iPos = 0) ;
                Function_Return sPath
            Move (Mid(sPath, (Length(sPath) - iPos), (iPos + 1))) to sLeaf
            If (SearchArray(Lowercase(sLeaf), asGeneric) = -1) ;
                Function_Return sPath
            Move (Left(sPath, (iPos - 1))) to sPath
        Loop
        Function_Return sPath
    End_Function

    /* The libraries among a set of SELECTED folders - the names of every library whose root folder
       contains at least one of them.

       Selecting a library folder is allowed (Nils 2026-07-31: "if the user selects a library folder,
       we act on it. We don't want to prohibit the user from it"), so nothing here excludes anything.
       This exists only so the pre-run gate can say WHICH libraries are about to be edited: the files
       are backed up either way, but the next update to the library upstream will overwrite or
       conflict with the changes, and that is not something a backup protects.

       Prefix test with a trailing separator on both sides, so "...\vwin32fh\" matches
       "...\vwin32fh\AppSrc" and not "...\vwin32fhExtra". */
    Function LibrariesInFolders String sSwsFile String[] ByRef asFolders Returns String[]
        tLibraryInfo[] aLibs
        String[] asHit
        String sRoot sSel
        Integer i iMax j jMax

        Get WorkspaceLibraryInventory sSwsFile "" to aLibs
        Move (SizeOfArray(aLibs) - 1) to iMax
        Move (SizeOfArray(asFolders) - 1) to jMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Get LibraryRootFolder aLibs[i].sFolder to sRoot
            Move (Lowercase(vFolderFormat(sRoot))) to sRoot
            For j from 0 to jMax
                Move (Lowercase(vFolderFormat(asFolders[j]))) to sSel
                If (Left(sSel, Length(sRoot)) = sRoot) Begin
                    If (SearchArray(aLibs[i].sName, asHit) = -1) ;
                        Move aLibs[i].sName to asHit[SizeOfArray(asHit)]
                End
            Loop
        Loop
        Function_Return asHit
    End_Function

    /* Where a library's current version lives, or "" for one we have no home for.

       Matched on the library's FOLDER, which the survey showed is the only stable identifier - the
       .sws filenames use four incompatible schemes and libraries rename their own between releases
       (DbUpdateFramework 19.1 -> DUFLibrary25.0). Exact match first, then a prefix, because SigCJ's
       folders carry their version in the name ("SigCodejock Wrapper 18.2.0") while everything else
       is a plain folder name. Prefix, not "contains": "duf" appears inside plenty of words.

       A SEED, not a settings file. Nine entries that change when a project moves house, which is
       rare; if customers start needing their own entries it should graduate to a versioned JSON
       beside DeepScanRules.json rather than grow here.

       Unknown libraries return "" and simply get no link - the report still lists them, which is
       the honest outcome for a library we cannot place.
    */
    Function LibraryHomeUrl String sName Returns String
        String sLow

        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sName))) to sLow
        If (sLow = "") ;
            Function_Return ""

        /* Wil van Antwerpen's project is the SOURCE OF TRUTH for vwin32fh - the releases happen
           there ("Adding a df26 sws file", rev 50). Nils also keeps a fork at
           github.com/NilsSve/Library-vwin32fh which is what DFRefactor itself consumes; the report
           points at upstream because it is answering "where does the current version live", which
           for a customer workspace is Wil's. Swap this if the fork should be the answer instead. */
        If (sLow = "vwin32fh") ;
            Function_Return "https://projects.vdf-guidance.com/projects/vwin32fh-windows-file-handling"
        /* The DF20 port is the LAST release - author-deprecated, nothing newer is coming. Home is
           the NilsSve build since 2026-09-05: DataFlexCode's repo bundles a 2003-era vWin32fh.pkg
           that shadows the vwin32fh package in every workspace carrying both; the build ships no
           copy and depends on the package instead (see CS_ApExampleVdfQuery in AddPackageFT.dg). */
        If (sLow = "vdfquery") ;
            Function_Return "https://github.com/NilsSve/VDFQuery20"
        If (Left(sLow, 11) = "sigcodejock" or sLow = "sigcj") ;
            Function_Return "https://subversion.asckey.co.uk/svn/sigcj/trunk/"
        If (sLow = "duf" or Left(sLow, 18) = "dbupdateframework") ;
            Function_Return "https://github.com/NilsSve/Library-DUF"
        If (sLow = "dfabout") ;
            Function_Return "https://github.com/NilsSve/Library-DFAbout"
        If (sLow = "rdctoolslib") ;
            Function_Return "https://github.com/NilsSve/Library-RDCToolsLib"
        If (sLow = "dfunit") ;
            Function_Return "https://github.com/NilsSve/Library-DFUnit"
        If (Left(sLow, 18) = "datafolderselector" or Left(sLow, 20) = "data folder selector") ;
            Function_Return "https://github.com/NilsSve/Library-DataFolderSelector"
        If (Left(sLow, 16) = "holidaysfunclib") ;
            Function_Return "https://github.com/NilsSve/Library-HolidaysFuncLib"
        Function_Return ""
    End_Function

    /* ---- THE INVENTORY ---------------------------------------------------------------------
       One record per library this workspace references, answering: to move to sTargetVersion,
       what is the state of each library? See tLibraryInfo / the eLib* enum in
       RefactorConstants.h.pkg.

       sTargetVersion "" (no compiler picked yet) still returns the inventory, with every status
       eLibUnknown - the caller can list what a workspace uses without pretending to a verdict. */
    /* EVERY [Libraries] entry of a workspace, in file order - including the ones pointing at a .sws
       that is not there.

       THE FULL ANSWER, and the one a screen should show. Its filtered sibling
       WorkspaceLibraryInventory drops unresolvable entries, which is right for a caller that needs
       a readable .sws (the copy plan's retargeting) and WRONG for anything that reports a count:
       a workspace declaring six libraries with two missing reported four, and the tile, the report
       footer and the row list all agreed with each other while disagreeing with the file. Only the
       ALL-missing case was ever caught (2026-08-05); this is the same lie in its partial form.

       A NOT-FOUND row carries what is knowable and nothing more: sRawEntry (what the file says),
       sRefSwsFile (where that resolves to, which is the diagnosis), sName from the file stem, and
       eLibNotFound. Version, available versions and target are left empty rather than guessed -
       there was nothing to read them from. */
    Function WorkspaceLibraryEntries String sSwsFile String sTargetVersion Returns tLibraryInfo[]
        tLibraryInfo[] aLibs
        String[] asRaw
        String sHome
        Integer iPos

        If (vFilePathExists(sSwsFile) = 0) ;
            Function_Return aLibs

        // The workspace's home is the folder its .sws sits in - derived, not read from
        // psHomeTarget, so this is pure with respect to its argument and testable against a
        // fixture workspace that is not the loaded one.
        Move sSwsFile to sHome
        Move (RightPos("\", sHome)) to iPos
        If (iPos = 0) ;
            Move (RightPos("/", sHome)) to iPos
        If (iPos = 0) ;
            Function_Return aLibs
        Move (Left(sHome, iPos)) to sHome

        Get SwsLibraryEntries sSwsFile to asRaw
        Get LibraryEntriesInfo (&asRaw) sHome sTargetVersion to aLibs
        Function_Return aLibs
    End_Function

    /* The same answer for a list of entries held in MEMORY rather than read from a file.

       THE EDITING CASE, and the reason this is split out. The Library Maintenance dialog shows a
       working list that Add and Remove have changed but OK has not yet written - so re-reading the
       .sws to describe it would describe the wrong list, and the row the user just added would
       render as though it were not there.

       Same rules, one implementation: WorkspaceLibraryEntries is now just "read the entries, derive
       the home, ask this". */
    Function LibraryEntriesInfo String[] ByRef asRaw String sHome String sTargetVersion Returns tLibraryInfo[]
        tLibraryInfo[] aLibs
        tLibraryInfo Lib
        tRFSearchResult[] aFound
        String sChildSws sFolder sVer sFileName
        Integer iChild iChildMax iFile iFileMax iPos iFmt

        Move (SizeOfArray(asRaw) - 1) to iChildMax
        For iChild from 0 to iChildMax
            Move (ResizeArray(Lib.asAvailVersions, 0)) to Lib.asAvailVersions
            Move asRaw[iChild] to Lib.sRawEntry
            Move "" to Lib.sRefVersion
            Move "" to Lib.sFolder
            Move "" to Lib.sTargetSwsFile
            Get DependencyKind asRaw[iChild] to Lib.eKind

            /* A SYSTEM PACKAGE IS NOT A PATH, and must never be resolved as one. Doing that is
               how "DataFlex-dev/Web UI FlexTron#1.0.51" came out as a red "not found - nothing is
               at that path": true of a file that was never meant to exist there.

               What is knowable comes from the entry itself and the global cache:
                 name    - the package name, spaces and all
                 version - PINNED BY THE CONSUMER, so it is what this workspace asked for rather
                           than what some file declares about itself
                 bFound  - is that exact version in the cache, i.e. is it installed here
               No target verdict: a package's DataFlex compatibility is the df-min/df-max range in
               its own package block, which nothing reads yet. eLibPackage says "this is a package"
               and stops there, which is the honest end of what we know. */
            If (Lib.eKind = eDepPackage) Begin
                Get PackageName asRaw[iChild] to Lib.sName
                Get PackageVersion asRaw[iChild] to Lib.sRefVersion
                Get PackageCacheFolder asRaw[iChild] to sFolder
                Move sFolder to Lib.sFolder
                Move "" to Lib.sRefSwsFile
                Move False to Lib.bFound
                If (sFolder <> "") Begin
                    // The package's own df.sws - its manifest, and where its chain continues.
                    Move (sFolder + "df.sws") to sChildSws
                    If (vFilePathExists(sChildSws) <> 0) Begin
                        Move sChildSws to Lib.sRefSwsFile
                        Move True to Lib.bFound
                    End
                End
                /* THE VERDICT, and the only sound one available for a package: its OWN declared
                   df-min / df-max, read from the df.sws inside its cache entry. Everything else
                   this class knows describes a .sws file, and a package is not one - which is why
                   the row had no verdict at all until the range could be read.

                   Three outcomes, and they are genuinely different:
                     not installed        - no file, so nothing to read and nothing claimed
                     installed, no range  - the package has not said; eLibPackage stands
                     installed, range     - a real answer, either way
                   Collapsing the middle into "incompatible" would put words in the package's
                   mouth. */
                Move eLibPackage to Lib.eStatus
                Move "" to Lib.sDfMin
                Move "" to Lib.sDfMax
                If (Lib.bFound = True and sTargetVersion <> "") Begin
                    Send SwsPackageDfRange Lib.sRefSwsFile (&Lib.sDfMin) (&Lib.sDfMax)
                    If (HasVersionRange(Self, Lib.sDfMin, Lib.sDfMax) = True) Begin
                        If (VersionInRange(Self, sTargetVersion, Lib.sDfMin, Lib.sDfMax) = False) ;
                            Move eLibPackageMismatch to Lib.eStatus
                    End
                End
                Move Lib to aLibs[SizeOfArray(aLibs)]
            End
            Else Begin
            Get ResolveLibraryPath asRaw[iChild] sHome to sChildSws
            Move sChildSws to Lib.sRefSwsFile
            Move (vFilePathExists(sChildSws) <> 0) to Lib.bFound
            Move eSwsMissing to iFmt
            If (Lib.bFound = True) ;
                Get SwsFormatOf sChildSws to iFmt
            If (Lib.bFound = False) Begin
                /* NOT FOUND. The name comes from the entry's own file stem - the folder cannot be
                   asked, because it may not exist either - and every version field stays empty
                   rather than being guessed at. eLibNotFound outranks the target verdicts: nothing
                   can be said about a library that could not be read. */
                Move (RightPos("\", asRaw[iChild])) to iPos
                If (iPos > 0) ;
                    Move (Mid(asRaw[iChild], (Length(asRaw[iChild]) - iPos), (iPos + 1))) to Lib.sName
                Else ;
                    Move asRaw[iChild] to Lib.sName
                Move eLibNotFound to Lib.eStatus
                Move Lib to aLibs[SizeOfArray(aLibs)]
            End
            Else If (iFmt = eSwsConflict) Begin
                /* THERE, BUT NOT A WORKSPACE FILE: git conflict markers (2026-09-14). Named from its
                   folder, which does exist; no version, no siblings, no target verdict - nothing read
                   from a file in that state could be trusted. See eLibUnreadable. */
                Move (RightPos("\", sChildSws)) to iPos
                Move (Left(sChildSws, iPos)) to sFolder
                Move sFolder to Lib.sFolder
                Get LibraryDisplayName sFolder to Lib.sName
                Move eLibUnreadable to Lib.eStatus
                Move Lib to aLibs[SizeOfArray(aLibs)]
            End
            Else Begin
                Get SwsDataFlexVersion sChildSws to Lib.sRefVersion
                Move (RightPos("\", sChildSws)) to iPos
                Move (Left(sChildSws, iPos)) to sFolder
                Move sFolder to Lib.sFolder
                Get LibraryDisplayName sFolder to Lib.sName

                // Sibling .sws files in the same folder = the versions this copy can offer.
                Move (rfFileSearch((sFolder + "*.sws"), DIRMODE_FILES_ONLY, False)) to aFound
                Move (SizeOfArray(aFound) - 1) to iFileMax
                For iFile from 0 to iFileMax
                    Move aFound[iFile].sFilename to sFileName
                    If (IsConsumableLibrarySws(Self, sFileName) = True) Begin
                        Get SwsDataFlexVersion (sFolder + sFileName) to sVer
                        If (sVer <> "") Begin
                            If (SearchArray(sVer, Lib.asAvailVersions) = -1) ;
                                Move sVer to Lib.asAvailVersions[SizeOfArray(Lib.asAvailVersions)]
                            If (sVer = sTargetVersion and Lib.sTargetSwsFile = "") ;
                                Move (sFolder + sFileName) to Lib.sTargetSwsFile
                        End
                    End
                Loop

                Move eLibUnknown to Lib.eStatus
                If (sTargetVersion <> "") Begin
                    If (Lib.sRefVersion = sTargetVersion) Begin
                        Move eLibMatchesTarget to Lib.eStatus
                    End
                    Else If (Lib.sTargetSwsFile <> "") Begin
                        Move eLibRetargetLocal to Lib.eStatus
                    End
                    Else Begin
                        Move eLibNoLocalBuild to Lib.eStatus
                    End
                End
                Move Lib to aLibs[SizeOfArray(aLibs)]
            End
            End    // ...of the eDepPath branch - a package took the arm above
        Loop
        Function_Return aLibs
    End_Function

    /* THE WHOLE CHAIN, depth-first, in declaration order - a workspace's libraries, then each of
       THEIR libraries beneath them.

       WHY THIS EXISTS. A library's [Properties] Version= is a claim about ITSELF and says nothing
       about what it in turn depends on. E:\WinServ (2026-08-12) is the case that named the problem:
       one declared library, WebAppSrc-Library-DF250.sws, Version=25.0 - and all seven of ITS
       dependencies pinned to DF191. The workspace showed a single row reading "25.0", while the
       build it produces pulls 19.1 library code into DataFlex 26. The sibling header from 2021
       shows what the convention should be: WebAppSrc-Library-DF141.sws pins all seven to DF141, so
       a version header is meant to carry a matching chain. That one had its version bumped and its
       chain left behind.

       A SEPARATE FUNCTION, deliberately - WorkspaceLibraryEntries stays flat. Two callers below it
       need exactly the workspace's OWN entries and would break silently if the chain appeared:
       _RetargetedLibraryEntry rewrites the entries of one .sws, and LibrariesInFolders matches
       selected folders against the libraries a workspace declares. Recursion is opt-in.

       CYCLE-SAFE by resolved path, not by name: two entries can name one file by different routes,
       and a library that (directly or otherwise) declares one of its own ancestors would otherwise
       walk forever. Depth-capped as a second belt - a chain deeper than a few levels is a
       configuration this tool should describe rather than exhaust itself on.

       The roll-up fields are filled from a row's IMMEDIATE children only, which is what makes the
       parent row able to say "declares 25.0, and its own seven are on 19.1" without the reader
       having to open the chain. */
    Function LibraryEntriesTree String[] ByRef asRaw String sHome String sTargetVersion Integer iDepth Integer iMaxDepth String[] ByRef asVisited Returns tLibraryInfo[]
        tLibraryInfo[] aOut aLevel aKids
        String[] asChild
        String sChildHome sKey sOldest
        Integer i iMax j jMax iAt iCount iBehind iKidVer iOldest

        Get LibraryEntriesInfo (&asRaw) sHome sTargetVersion to aLevel
        Move (SizeOfArray(aLevel) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move iDepth to aLevel[i].iDepth
            Move aLevel[i] to aOut[SizeOfArray(aOut)]
            Move (SizeOfArray(aOut) - 1) to iAt

            // Nothing below an entry that is not there or cannot be read, and nothing below the depth cap.
            If (aLevel[i].bFound = True and aLevel[i].eStatus <> eLibUnreadable and iDepth < iMaxDepth) Begin
                // Visited by RESOLVED path, lower-cased: one file reached two ways is one file.
                Move (Lowercase(aLevel[i].sRefSwsFile)) to sKey
                If (SearchArray(sKey, asVisited) = -1) Begin
                    Move sKey to asVisited[SizeOfArray(asVisited)]
                    Get SwsLibraryEntries aLevel[i].sRefSwsFile to asChild
                    If (SizeOfArray(asChild) > 0) Begin
                        Get _FolderOf aLevel[i].sRefSwsFile to sChildHome
                        Get LibraryEntriesTree (&asChild) sChildHome sTargetVersion (iDepth + 1) iMaxDepth (&asVisited) to aKids

                        /* ROLL UP THE IMMEDIATE CHILDREN onto the row just appended. Only those at
                           the next depth: a grandchild is the child's business to report, and
                           counting it here would make one late library look like several. */
                        Move 0 to iCount
                        Move 0 to iBehind
                        Move "" to sOldest
                        Move 0 to iOldest
                        Move (SizeOfArray(aKids) - 1) to jMax
                        For j from 0 to jMax
                            If (aKids[j].iDepth = (iDepth + 1)) Begin
                                Increment iCount
                                If (aKids[j].sRefVersion <> "") Begin
                                    If (IsVersionBelow(Self, aKids[j].sRefVersion, aLevel[i].sRefVersion) = True) ;
                                        Increment iBehind
                                    Get _VersionKey aKids[j].sRefVersion to iKidVer
                                    If (iKidVer > 0) Begin
                                        If (iOldest = 0 or iKidVer < iOldest) Begin
                                            Move iKidVer to iOldest
                                            Move aKids[j].sRefVersion to sOldest
                                        End
                                    End
                                End
                            End
                        Loop
                        Move iCount   to aOut[iAt].iChainCount
                        Move iBehind  to aOut[iAt].iChainBehind
                        Move sOldest  to aOut[iAt].sChainOldest

                        // The children follow their parent immediately, which is what makes the
                        // flat array render as a tree without anyone having to sort it.
                        Move (SizeOfArray(aKids) - 1) to jMax
                        For j from 0 to jMax
                            // Only the IMMEDIATE children are named by this row; deeper ones were
                            // already stamped by their own parent one level down.
                            // Begin/End, not a continuation: two statements, and a ";" would
                            // attach only the first.
                            If (aKids[j].iDepth = (iDepth + 1)) Begin
                                Move aLevel[i].sName       to aKids[j].sDeclaredBy
                                Move aLevel[i].sRefSwsFile to aKids[j].sDeclaredBySws
                            End
                            Move aKids[j] to aOut[SizeOfArray(aOut)]
                        Loop
                    End
                End
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return aOut
    End_Function

    // The chain for a workspace FILE - LibraryEntriesTree's counterpart to WorkspaceLibraryEntries.
    Function WorkspaceLibraryTree String sSwsFile String sTargetVersion Integer iMaxDepth Returns tLibraryInfo[]
        tLibraryInfo[] aLibs
        String[] asRaw asVisited
        String sHome
        Integer iPos

        If (vFilePathExists(sSwsFile) = 0) ;
            Function_Return aLibs
        Move sSwsFile to sHome
        Move (RightPos("\", sHome)) to iPos
        If (iPos = 0) ;
            Move (RightPos("/", sHome)) to iPos
        If (iPos = 0) ;
            Function_Return aLibs
        Move (Left(sHome, iPos)) to sHome

        // The workspace itself is the first visited node, so a library declaring it back is a cycle.
        Move (Lowercase(sSwsFile)) to asVisited[0]
        Get SwsLibraryEntries sSwsFile to asRaw
        Get LibraryEntriesTree (&asRaw) sHome sTargetVersion 0 iMaxDepth (&asVisited) to aLibs
        Function_Return aLibs
    End_Function

    /* The RESOLVABLE entries only - the original contract, now a filter over WorkspaceLibraryEntries
       so there is one walk of the .sws and one set of rules.

       KEPT, rather than folded away, because two callers genuinely need a .sws they can read:
       _RetargetedLibraryEntry rewrites an entry by looking at the versions beside the one it points
       at, and LibrariesInFolders matches selected folders against a library's root - neither has any
       use for a row whose folder does not exist. Anything that REPORTS should call
       WorkspaceLibraryEntries instead, or it will count what it can see rather than what is
       declared. */
    Function WorkspaceLibraryInventory String sSwsFile String sTargetVersion Returns tLibraryInfo[]
        tLibraryInfo[] aAll aFoundOnly
        Integer i iMax

        Get WorkspaceLibraryEntries sSwsFile sTargetVersion to aAll
        Move (SizeOfArray(aAll) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            // ...nor one that is there but cannot be read (git conflict markers, 2026-09-14).
            If (aAll[i].bFound = True and aAll[i].eStatus <> eLibUnreadable) ;
                Move aAll[i] to aFoundOnly[SizeOfArray(aFoundOnly)]
        Loop
        Function_Return aFoundOnly
    End_Function

    /* The package a source line USES, lower-cased and always carrying an extension - or "" when the
       line is not a Use statement. The whole point of pulling this out is that it is PURE: no disk,
       no workspace, no app, so it can be unit-tested against the awkward forms directly.

       What counts as a Use, and why:
         - Case-insensitive, and the keyword must be followed by whitespace. "Used" and "UseThing"
           are not Use statements, and a line starting with "//" is a comment whatever follows.
         - A trailing "// ..." comment is cut before the name is read - "Use vWin32fh.pkg  // legacy"
           is one dependency, not a package called "vWin32fh.pkg  // legacy".
         - Quotes are stripped: both Use vWin32fh.pkg and Use "vWin32fh.pkg" are legal.
         - A name with NO dot gets ".pkg" appended, because that is what the compiler assumes and
           what the file on disk is called - so "Use vWin32fh" and "Use vWin32fh.pkg" compare equal.
           A name that already has one keeps it, which is what makes RefactorConstants.h.pkg match
           itself rather than a phantom "RefactorConstants.h.pkg.pkg".

       NOT handled, deliberately: a Use inside a block comment. Recognising those needs the lexer's
       comment state, this runs over raw lines, and a Use commented out with a block is rare enough
       that carrying a whole lexer here would cost more than the false positive. */
    Function UsedPackageOnLine String sLine Returns String
        String sWork sName sSep
        Integer iPos

        Move (Trim(sLine)) to sWork
        If (sWork = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        If (Left(sWork, 2) = "//") ;
            Function_Return ""
        If (Lowercase(Left(sWork, 3)) <> "use") ;
            Function_Return ""
        If (Length(sWork) < 5) ;
            Function_Return ""
        // Position 4 must SEPARATE the keyword from its argument - space or tab. Without this test
        // "UseTheThing" reads as a Use of "TheThing".
        Move (Mid(sWork, 1, 4)) to sSep
        If (sSep <> " " and sSep <> (Character(9))) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (Trim(Mid(sWork, (Length(sWork) - 3), 4))) to sName
        Move (Pos("//", sName)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 0) ;
            Move (Trim(Left(sName, (iPos - 1)))) to sName
        Move (Replaces('"', sName, "")) to sName
        Move (Trim(sName)) to sName
        If (sName = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        // A bare name means the compiler's default extension; make it explicit so the comparison is
        // filename-to-filename.
        If (Pos(".", sName) = 0) ;
            Move (sName + ".pkg") to sName
        Function_Return (Lowercase(sName))
    End_Function

    /* Every .pkg FILENAME a library folder offers, lower-cased, recursively - the set a consumer's
       Use statement has to name to be depending on this library.

       Filenames, not class names: a Use names a file, so comparing filenames is exact, needs no
       parsing of the library's own source, and cannot be fooled by a class that two libraries both
       define. Recursive because a library's packages are routinely a folder down (vwin32fh keeps
       its own under StudioLibrary\). */
    Function LibraryPackageNames String sLibFolder Returns String[]
        tRFSearchResult[] aFiles
        String[] asNames
        String sMask sBase
        Integer i iMax iPos

        If (sLibFolder = "") ;
            Function_Return asNames
        Move (vFolderFormat(sLibFolder)) to sLibFolder
        If (rfDirectoryExists(sLibFolder) = False) ;
            Function_Return asNames
        Move (sLibFolder + "*.pkg") to sMask
        Get rfFileSearchRecursive sMask DIRMODE_FILES_ONLY False to aFiles
        Move (SizeOfArray(aFiles) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (RightPos("\", aFiles[i].sFilename)) to iPos
            If (iPos > 0) ;
                Move (Mid(aFiles[i].sFilename, (Length(aFiles[i].sFilename) - iPos), (iPos + 1))) to sBase
            Else ;
                Move aFiles[i].sFilename to sBase
            Move (Lowercase(Trim(sBase))) to sBase
            If (sBase <> "" and (SearchArray(sBase, asNames)) = -1) ;
                Move sBase to asNames[SizeOfArray(asNames)]
        Loop
        Function_Return asNames
    End_Function

End_Class

/*  WHICH INVENTORY CLASS TO CREATE (2026-10-05).

    cLibraryInventory reads no libraries from a JSON workspace file: SwsJsonLibraryEntries is a hook
    that only an application-side subclass can fill, because it needs the application's reader and
    this package must also work in the console test runner. Code that is compiled into every
    program - the analyzer's library roots, the engine's note before a run - cannot name that
    subclass, created this class by name, and was blind on every DataFlex 26 workspace: no row was
    ever "a library's", the note never showed, and the Libraries dialog counted no compiler message
    against any library. Found while a hint for a missing include file would not show (Nils).

    So the class to create is a variable. It holds this class here; the package that defines a
    subclass able to read a JSON file sets it (cFTLibraryReport.pkg: cLibraryInventoryApp). Wherever
    a WORKSPACE's libraries are wanted:   Get Create giLibraryInventoryClass to ho
    A host without such a package - the test runner - gets this class, as before. Code that only
    needs the path arithmetic or SwsFormatOf can go on creating this class by name. */
Global_Variable Integer giLibraryInventoryClass
Move (RefClass(cLibraryInventory)) to giLibraryInventoryClass

/*  The library CHAIN of a workspace, in one call.

    Convenience only - the logic is WorkspaceLibraryTree on the class above, and it stays there on
    purpose. Nothing here belongs on cRefactorApplication: UnitTests builds a plain
    cApplication, so a function living on the application object is unreachable from the suite and
    its tests would pass or fail for reasons unrelated to the logic (see this file's header, and
    cDeepScanner for the same pattern). This wrapper gives the one-line call site without paying
    that price - it creates a cLibraryInventory, asks it, and destroys it.

        Get WorkspaceLibraryChain sSws sTargetVersion to aLibs

    Walks to CI_LibraryTreeMaxDepth, the shared depth the Libraries report and the copy plan both
    use. Pass "" for sTargetVersion when the question is "what does this workspace declare" rather
    than "is each entry built for the target" - the per-entry verdict (eStatus) needs a version, the
    structure does not.

    Each row's bFound says whether the .sws it points at is really on disk; rows are returned
    whether or not they resolve, because a declaration pointing at nothing is usually the very thing
    the caller wants to know about.

    NOT for a paint path. It re-reads the chain every call - a directory search plus a read of every
    sibling .sws per library. Callers that render repeatedly should memoise, as
    cFTLibraryReport.RefreshLibraryCounts does; callers that already ARE a cLibraryInventory (or a
    subclass) should call WorkspaceLibraryTree on themselves instead of creating a second object.  */
Function WorkspaceLibraryChain Global String sSwsFile String sTargetVersion Returns tLibraryInfo[]
    Handle hoInv
    tLibraryInfo[] aLibs

    Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventory)) to hoInv
    Get WorkspaceLibraryTree of hoInv sSwsFile sTargetVersion CI_LibraryTreeMaxDepth to aLibs
    Send Destroy of hoInv
    Function_Return aLibs
End_Function
